window.harnessChunks["rows-672bfacb3e84d2d3.js"]=["<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-29T11:10:59+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ayandexyz/Honey-I-Shrunk-the-Harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Histh</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-29\">2026-10-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ayandexyz/Honey-I-Shrunk-the-Harness/tree/f2aff641e55c2e6bb8c55a8fb82b67bdf2addd1a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision f2aff641e55c2e6bb8c55a8fb82b67bdf2addd1a\">f2aff64 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Repository task transformation by model-directed coding agents. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Different user requests, current source state, command errors, child results and repair feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose concrete tools/arguments/delegation and revise work from results. - Decision owner: Configured root/child model actors under their inherited permissions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Provider driver, tool registry, session store, execution envelope, shell/files, queue/batch planner and run caps. - Closure path: Provider tool proposals execute and their model-visible results append to history; subsequent provider turns select further actions or a final answer. - Boundary reachability: The shipped core CLI builds AgentLoop and registers real file/shell/agent tools; it does not merely instruct an outside coding agent to do the work. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Without the model, transport/storage/gates do not choose task-specific file changes or repair decisions. - Evidence: agent/loop.ts; tools/agent.ts; cli/create-cli.ts - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Prevent shared-tree mutation races among synchronous delegated work units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parallel writer agents can race on the same project tree; readonly child operations can safely overlap. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select readonly parallel batching versus exclusive synchronous writer execution. - Decision owner: Developer-selected concurrencySafeFor and runtime batch protocol; no autonomous inter-agent coordinator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent readOnly defaults, explore-mode tool removal, per-call safety classification and awaited sequential/parallel execution. - Closure path: A writer delegate&#39;s tool batch completes before subsequent batches proceed; readonly results are collected together and returned to the parent model before its next step. - Boundary reachability: Agent tool supplies concurrencySafeFor(readOnly); AgentLoop uses the actual planToolBatches result to await calls, and child mode physically withholds mutating tools for readonly work. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: C marks the supplied interference-specific path. The model selects work, but does not own the serialization or change a joint coordination policy. - Evidence: tools/agent.ts; tools/batching.ts; agent/loop.ts; permission/mode-policy.ts - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high for synchronous delegation - Caveats: Background writable delegates are outside this mutual-exclusion guarantee; no cross-process/global shared-file reservation or complete semantic merge protocol is claimed. - Distinct S1 units: Independently tasked root and child model work units; multiple readonly delegates may run concurrently. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Concurrent writable subagents operating on the same tree can race, explicitly identified in the agent tool&#39;s concurrency contract. - Attenuating coordination relation: Readonly agents share safe batches; readOnly:false agents run in an exclusive awaited batch, with readonly modes physically excluding edit/write/bash. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Parent waits for the writer result before later batch/model decisions; readonly evidence is merged into returned tool results. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The scheduling distinction is explicitly tied to write interference and enforced through the child tool set and per-call safety, rather than credited merely from delegation.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: Whole-night-run current acceptance, budget and intervention control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Iterations can spend a shared run budget, repeatedly fail, produce unverifiable changes or leave uncommitted/unsafe work across successive task cells. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Continue/retry/wait/stop the run and commit versus reset iteration work according to its current aggregate state and selected verification constraints. - Decision owner: Developer-authored runNight state machine under operator-supplied goals, bounds and verify criterion; no autonomous regulatory policy selector established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Manifest/usage records, run limits, Git commit/reset, notes, verification command and quota-wait plan. - Closure path: Current outcome/usage/Git/check results select commit/reset/repair/stop; failure notes and repairPending return into the next iteration, or termination prevents further spending. - Boundary reachability: freecode night CLI imports runNight/runIteration and wires real Git, provider/agent iteration, clock/store and operator controls. Injected dependencies are supplied by the shipped CLI, not only test fixtures. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Runtime can enforce a preselected regulatory policy, but neither the budget monitor nor an advisory model supervisor owns the underlying shared commitments. C records this S3-specific constructor path; no agent-owned policy optimization is inferred. - Evidence: autonomous/orchestrator.ts; cli/commands/night-run.ts; autonomous/iteration.ts; autonomous/budget.ts - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high within one bounded night run - Caveats: Not whole-project/organization governance. Upfront user limits and a cancel/status command do not independently establish a closed parent S3 mode. - Whole-system current view: The night controller aggregates iteration number, cumulative USD, consecutive failures, quota-wait allowance, verification outcome and current dirty Git state for the entire declared run. - Current-control decision scope: Accept/discard work, allocate another iteration or retry, carry repair notes, stop on current resource/commitment bounds and refuse unverifiable commits.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Complementary direct-artifact challenge of a coding agent&#39;s completion claim. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A nontrivial change may be broken, fabricated or technically present but unreachable despite ordinary typecheck/test success. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Assign PASS/FAIL/PARTIAL after independently checking changed source against the original request and actual entry-to-effect reachability. - Decision owner: Fresh read-only verifier model; parent cannot self-assign its verdict. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Changed-file list, original task, optional observed tests/prior report, bounded isolated child loop, verdict parser and capped repair gate. - Closure path: AgentLoop invokes verifyChanges after at least three changed files; FAIL report becomes a pending reminder, advances to another operational model turn, and can be rechecked up to two attempts. - Boundary reachability: The supplied core completion path directly calls verifyChanges; executeSubagent constructs the verifier&#39;s separate loop/read-only identity with a narrower inherited envelope. Bench/eval judges and contributor agents are not used. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The verifier judges raw source and reachable behavior using its own reads; deterministic tests/parsing merely support or enforce that judgment. - Evidence: agent/subagent.ts; agent/loop.ts; permission/mode-policy.ts - Basis: structural - Confidence: high for bounded source path - Caveats: Same provider/model may be shared; verifier receives task/files/test/prior-audit context, not unrestricted institutional independence. Gate is thresholded/capped and PARTIAL can proceed; no audit-effectiveness claim. - Claim being audited: Main coding work is complete, correct and actually wired into requested behavior. - Ordinary reporting path: Root model&#39;s final no-tool answer and routine deterministic typecheck/test evidence. - Complementary access path: Fresh read-only verifier reads changed source, callers and user entry-point reachability and can contradict the parent&#39;s success claim. - Independence boundary: Separate generated session/context and read-only actor; parent cannot choose its verdict. Verification disables nested completion features to avoid recursive self-audit. - Who acts on findings: Root operational agent receives FAIL findings and performs another fix/review cycle under the runtime cap.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: External/prospective capability adaptation is not established at this harness boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Memory extraction/consolidation and graphs preserve experience; advisory redirection proposes task routes; night iterations improve a user-selected project objective. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decisive decision and return path supplied. - Decision owner: No credited owner; operator/developer configuration and task-level model decisions remain distinct. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Permissions, hooks, provider/model configuration, memory state and runtime enforcement. - Closure path: No qualifying function-specific metasystem closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Memory extraction/consolidation and graphs preserve experience; advisory redirection proposes task routes; night iterations improve a user-selected project objective. - Evidence: memory/consolidate.ts; agent/redirect/supervisor.ts; autonomous/orchestrator.ts - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Identity/ultimate-policy closure is not established at this harness boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Mode policies, immutable child ceilings, allow/ask/deny rules, night safety constraints and ordinary user approvals regulate task rights. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decisive decision and return path supplied. - Decision owner: No credited owner; operator/developer configuration and task-level model decisions remain distinct. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Permissions, hooks, provider/model configuration, memory state and runtime enforcement. - Closure path: No qualifying function-specific metasystem closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Mode policies, immutable child ceilings, allow/ask/deny rules, night safety constraints and ordinary user approvals regulate task rights. - Evidence: permission/mode-policy.ts; permission/envelope.ts; autonomous/envelope.ts - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/39d7c111f787d4a78b43cdbb06e1a68801359193/assessments/hi/histh.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 39d7c111f787d4a78b43cdbb06e1a68801359193\">39d7c11 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-03T18:39:20+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/mindweave-cli/mindweave\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Mindweave</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-03\">2026-10-03</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/mindweave-cli/mindweave/tree/7a108f043dbfca273dd420d193803f41040a64b7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7a108f043dbfca273dd420d193803f41040a64b7\">7a108f0 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform environment-facing software work by inspecting repository state, choosing tools, editing code, executing commands/tests and reacting to returned evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: arbitrary codebase structure, failing checks, diagnostics, command output, provider variation, tool errors, user feedback and implementation alternatives. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next substantive coding/search/command action, interpret returned results, revise the approach, delegate bounded work and decide when to report. - Decision owner: the active model-backed Mindweave actor and, within assigned scope, each child model-backed subagent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider-neutral engine; tool registry; deterministic guards; project memory/governor injection; file/shell/MCP tools; session persistence; language-server diagnostics; budgets and cancellation. - Closure path: user/project state → model request → selected tool/action → runtime executes/refuses → result returns into the transcript → later model turn changes subsequent work. - Boundary reachability: this is the installed terminal agent&#39;s normal runtime; spawnsubagent reuses the same first-party engine for bounded children. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model actor leaves deterministic tools and guards but removes the open-ended judgment that chooses and interprets coding actions. - Evidence: README.md; src/dynamo/engine.ts; src/tools/subagent.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external provider inference is a dependency; the credited role/tool/feedback loop is first-party.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: prevent destructive interference between concurrently delegated S1 units by allowing parallel read-only work while serializing mutating child agents. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two child coding agents editing the same project concurrently can race, overwrite changes or reason from incompatible intermediate filesystem states. - Decisive decision or feedback right: classify a delegated worker as read-only versus mutating and apply the corresponding parallel-versus-serial execution lane. - Decision owner: constructor/runtime path. The parent model supplies readonly, but Mindweave&#39;s first-party scheduler owns the hard rule that only read-only workers are concurrency-safe and mutating workers run alone. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: process-wide semaphore; per-tool isConcurrencySafe; child-session forking; one-level recursion cap; step budgets. - Closure path: multiple delegated children are requested → runtime inspects the child mode → read-only workers may execute concurrently while a mutating worker is serialized → subsequent child filesystem actions cannot race with another editing child → results return to the parent. - Boundary reachability: this policy is implemented directly in shipped spawnsubagent and used by the standard engine&#39;s concurrent tool execution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the collision attenuation is guaranteed by constructor/runtime scheduling rather than an autonomous actor negotiating between workers; the first-party S2-specific path is nevertheless complete enough to meet the constructor threshold. - Evidence: src/tools/subagent.ts; src/dynamo/engine.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic delegation and the semaphore capacity ceiling are not the witness; the positive mapping is the explicit edit-race prevention rule. - Distinct S1 units: multiple forked Mindweave child agents, each running the same model/tool engine on a bounded project task. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent mutating children can race on shared project files and invalidate each other&#39;s working state. - Attenuating coordination relation: runtime admits parallelism only for read-only children and routes mutating children through a serial execution lane. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the scheduling classification determines whether a child may begin concurrently and therefore what filesystem state its later tool calls can observe/mutate. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the implementation explicitly names concurrent edit races as the disturbance and changes execution concurrency specifically to eliminate that interference.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: regulate the mission&#39;s current commitments through a user-approved whole plan that binds subsequent execution and returns failed-plan decisions to the user rather than allowing silent replanning. - Disturbance / variety regulated: an autonomous coding actor can drift from an agreed implementation plan, expand scope, or improvise around a failing current step in ways that change the user&#39;s active commitments without authority. - Decisive decision or feedback right: approve the full plan that governs the work and, when execution evidence shows the approved path is no longer viable, decide whether/how to replan. - Decision owner: the user/operator as legitimate parent authority in the supported plan mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: read-only plan mode; exitplan; persisted .mindweave/plan.md; standing-knowledge injection; repeat-failure/divergence detector; automatic clearing/completion of the plan artifact after the governed work turn. - Closure path: model researches and proposes a full plan → user approves → runtime persists exact approved plan and injects it into execution → model must follow the approved commitments → repeated plan-step failure triggers a hard stop with instruction not to improvise → user receives the unresolved current-control choice and can authorize a new plan. - Boundary reachability: plan mode and plan approval are standard interactive Mindweave features; the approved artifact is read by the same runtime that executes the work, including across sessions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model proposes and executes, but the authority to establish or replace the whole approved commitment set belongs to the user in this mode. - Evidence: src/dynamo/engine.ts; src/dynamo/planArtifact.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: ordinary user prompts/approvals are not credited. The witness is the distinct plan mode with whole-plan authority, persisted returned commitment and fail-closed replan path. - Whole-system current view: the complete approved implementation plan is the mission-level set of current commitments presented to the parent before execution; the runtime retains that exact plan as standing knowledge while work proceeds. - Current-control decision scope: establish/replace the mission&#39;s current implementation commitments and decide what to do when the active approved plan cannot be followed. - Parent mode: user approval creates the active plan; repeated execution failure returns the decision to that same parent authority; the returned plan governs later operation. - No autonomous/constructor base mode is claimed: todo/re-scope/verification guards support current execution, but this review does not treat those fixed backstops or model-authored task lists as an independently established S3 ownership mode.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge completed code/work through a separate adversarial verifier agent before its verdict is relied on. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a worker/main coding actor can produce plausible but incorrect changes, skip meaningful checks or self-report success without evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge the already-produced work as pass, fail or partial after independently executing checks and attempting to break it. - Decision owner: a separate model-backed verifier child created with verify:true and verifier-specific instructions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: read-only child tool restriction; separate child session/context; verifier system prompt; required command/output report shape; deterministic evidence counter that downgrades an unevidenced pass. - Closure path: parent identifies work to audit → launches adversarial verifier with original request/change scope → verifier independently reads/runs checks without edit authority → verifier returns evidence-backed verdict → parser may downgrade unsupported pass → parent receives verdict/concerns and can fix, continue or accept. - Boundary reachability: verifier mode is a documented option of the shipped spawnsubagent tool, not a test-only evaluator. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the substantive adversarial test selection and pass/fail judgment are made by a separate model actor; deterministic parsing only enforces evidence hygiene. - Evidence: src/tools/subagent.ts; src/tools/verifyReport.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the ordinary deterministic VERIFYNUDGE is not the S3 witness; it only detects “edited but no check”. The positive mapping relies on the separate adversarial child. - Claim being audited: that already-performed code changes satisfy the original request and survive meaningful execution/boundary checks. - Ordinary reporting path: main/worker coding actor edits and reports its result. - Complementary access path: verifier child is forked in a separate session with a dedicated adversarial prompt and read-only tool set, then runs its own checks against project reality. - Independence boundary: the verifier cannot edit the project, does not share the parent&#39;s conversational reasoning and must produce command/output evidence; an unevidenced pass is mechanically rejected as partial. - Who acts on findings: the parent Mindweave actor receives the verdict and concerns and can take corrective operational action.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party prospective adaptation loop was established at the selected mission recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established at S4 level. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. Cross-session memory, project notes, web/MCP access and session summaries preserve useful information, but no shipped S4 actor was found that develops adaptation options from future/external distinctions and changes persistent organizational capability/strategy. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: savememory; automatic memory index; MINDWEAVE.md; project/directory notes; web/MCP tools; session memory/compaction; provider/model switching. - Closure path: not applicable; no distinct external-and-prospective intelligence → adaptation option → current capability/S3 return loop was established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the operational actor can remember facts and use them later, but durable context is not by itself prospective organizational adaptation. - Evidence: src/memory/autoMemory.ts; src/memory/projectNotes.ts; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: learning/persistence may improve later S1 decisions; that effect does not satisfy the stronger S4 functional/closure threshold.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no distinct first-party identity/ultimate-policy issue-resolution loop was established at the selected mission recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established at S5 level. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established as an S5 right. The user can state durable governor rules/forbidden paths/commands and the runtime enforces them, but these are general standing constraints rather than evidence of a distinct identity/ultimate-policy issue reaching a legitimate S5 authority. - Decision owner: not established for an S5 function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: governor rule files; forbid/unforbid tools; live governance reload; mechanical path/command/MCP enforcement; project notes and skills. - Closure path: no qualifying identity/ultimate-policy issue → authoritative decision → returned governance loop was established. User rule requests do return into operation, but the reviewed examples/path are ordinary project execution constraints. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: governor comments explicitly say the user decides what to make a rule/forbid and tools only record/enforce it; enforcement authority does not itself establish S5. - Evidence: src/tools/governorTools.ts; src/dynamo/engine.ts; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a deployment that uses the governor for genuine mission identity/ultimate-policy decisions could motivate a different recursion-specific analysis; generic standing rules are not enough here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/7c3e890b8e2bb1c88e87c4c4988e1cbba3aaf50e/assessments/mindweave.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 7c3e890b8e2bb1c88e87c4c4988e1cbba3aaf50e\">7c3e890 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-23T18:58:06+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/KarmSakha/karmx-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">karmX</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-23\">2026-10-23</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/KarmSakha/karmx-agent/tree/34a1f940e185db7dbfedba87be8206705e79b89d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 34a1f940e185db7dbfedba87be8206705e79b89d\">34a1f94 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously execute repository-development tasks through a provider/model/tool loop, with optional independently deciding subagent and top-level worker sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: project contents, shell errors, tool denials, web UI state, prompt uncertainty, model feedback and asynchronous work results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the LLM chooses which tool calls and arguments to issue and how to adapt after returned observations; separate delegate sessions follow their own model loops. - Decision owner: model-backed main karmX agent and spawned model-backed delegates/top-level sessions in supported modes. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider adapter, tool calls, Rust execution, permission modes, shell/browser/context platform extensions, persistent conversations, compaction and summon task metadata. - Closure path: incoming development request → provider chooses tools/sidekick tasks → runtime executes and returns result or denial → model chooses subsequent code changes, checks or user response. - Boundary reachability: compiled CLI connects its agent/runtime and enabled platform-extension registry; browser/context and summon entries are first-party default-enabled, while orchestrator can additionally create top-level S1s in an explicitly enabled mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model selects task-specific repository changes and revises them from tool feedback; deterministic execution and permissions only constrain those choices. - Evidence: agent.rs, statemachine/opstoolcalling.rs, summon.rs. - Basis: explicit operating docs and frozen source; no live LLM run. - Confidence: high for architecture and reachable decision path. - Caveats: no model correctness or practical coding quality is inferred from source and deterministic tests.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: distinct model-backed delegates can coexist, but no complete first-party interference-specific coordination loop is evidenced for their concurrent repository writes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: sidekick/delegates sharing files can edit the same path and conflict; the source explicitly warns this can happen. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the delegate tool advises the main model to partition files, but neither tracks actual file ownership nor mediates overlap or returns a collision-specific coordination response. - Decision owner: parent model may choose task decomposition in normal S1 work; no independently established S2 ownership closure. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: delegate/sidekick, load, asynchronous task handles, separate model contexts and tool-description advice to avoid same-file work. - Closure path: child summaries return to the parent for task synthesis, but no first-party inter-S1 conflict detection/negotiation/reservation → decision → adapted sibling operation loop is exposed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the advisory sentence can influence a model, but the checked delegate path neither carries resolved per-child file scopes nor traces conflict-specific feedback that changes other child operations. Top-level orchestrator messaging is generic transport absent a particular interference-regulation use. - Evidence: summon.rs, localfusion/mod.rs, orchestrator.rs. - Basis: source explicitly describes the collision risk and advisory-only response. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: users can voluntarily partition tasks or use separate worktrees, but no sufficient first-party specific closure is evidenced in this frozen implementation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: coordinate current commitments and execution capacity over several independently operating top-level agent sessions in an explicitly enabled first-party orchestrator mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: disparate session progress, idle/busy workers, current work commitments, agent capacity and interruption/reassignment needs. - Whole-system current view: orchestrator.listsessions surfaces session types, status, latest updates and message counts, and viewsession exposes current conversation evidence or a summary; the session manager spans the local current agent cohort. - Current-control decision scope: top-level orchestrating model chooses which new session to start, what message/assignment to send to an existing session and when to interrupt a busy one. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the model sees current agent roster/status and decides interventions over active peer work; not merely an automatic concurrency quota. - Decision owner: model-backed top-level agent in optional orchestrator-enabled mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: session manager, registered orchestrator tools, agent manager, start/interrupt permission checks, cancellation tokens, provider/session binding and response delivery. - Closure path: model inspects current roster/conversation → chooses start/message/interrupt tool → runtime creates or changes running sessions → subsequent operations follow new assignment or cancellation → model can reinspect current state. - Boundary reachability: platformextensions/mod.rs registers orchestrator as a first-party extension even though defaultenabled: false and hidden: true; normal platform extension configuration permits the supported opt-in mode. No external custom orchestrator actor or implementation is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the model&#39;s discretionary choice about which session to affect, the runtime only lists/enforces requested actions; it cannot select current priorities/commitments on its own. The optional path is genuine autonomous current control, unlike default lead/sidekick delegation by itself. - Evidence: orchestrator.rs, platformextensions/mod.rs, config/extensions.rs. - Basis: structural, function-specific top-level cross-session control; the frozen Goose-derived first-party constructor is retained by this fork. - Confidence: high for advertised first-party optional wiring, with opt-in scope explicitly bounded. - Caveats: ordinary default CLI operation does not automatically activate this mode; S3=A records supported availability, not usage in every local run.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no established independent complementary audit judgment returns into whole-current control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: errors or inaccurate delegated coding reports can require review, but a separate first-party audit governance path is not wired. - Decisive decision or feedback right: lead guidance asks to inspect the sidekick&#39;s diff, yet the long SIDEKICKPROMPT audit-like template is not consumed by the operating agent; regular tests and browser previews are direct work verification. - Decision owner: no distinct independent S3 audit actor established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: file/diff tools, sidekick report contract, model tool results, user review of artifacts and unit/eval scripts. - Closure path: sidekick results or browser DOM observations return to the producing/lead S1 decision path; no independent complementary sampler → review finding → S3 corrective return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: asking the same lead to read its worker&#39;s diff is not an evidentially independent complementary audit circuit, and unmounted template text cannot supply ownership. - Evidence: localfusion/mod.rs, summon.rs, orchestrator.rs. - Basis: source-level audit-role and reachable-path inspection. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a separately configured auditor could close this function downstream, but cannot be borrowed into the shipped runtime assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party forward-looking external environmental model driving durable organizational capability redesign is closed. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing code state, browsers and user prompts present current-work uncertainties rather than a monitored future strategic environment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the model selects retrieval/enhancement/rerank for the present request, while human config chooses providers and extension use; no future-oriented adaptation option receives organizational approval/return. - Decision owner: no S4-specific prospective choice owner established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: context engine&#39;s workspace retrieval/relevance ranking, prompt enhancer, browser preview DOM/console, Chrome CDP control, predictive context compaction, user-maintained skills and provider configuration. - Closure path: context/browser observations can revise the current coding turn; they do not form an outside-and-then scan → adaptation option → changed reusable organizational capability → present-control loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: even model-backed prompt enhancement and browser inspection improve present task execution, not necessarily the future operating identity/capabilities of the harness. - Evidence: contextengine/mod.rs, browserpreview/mod.rs, computercontrol/mod.rs. - Basis: bounded built-in capabilities/source and documentation. - Confidence: high for lack of proven prospective capability closure in inspected modes. - Caveats: an operator can ask the coding agent to build a new capability, but this remains external task assignment rather than an S4-specific organizational adaptive function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no legitimate ultimate-policy or organizational identity decision path is established in the operating multi-session agent. - Disturbance / variety regulated: per-tool safety/permissions and session authority matter, but are tactical task constraints rather than identity-level disputes between S3 current control and S4 adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model can issue task/tool requests, permission manager and human operator choose whether particular actions are allowed; code enforces session relation bounds. - Decision owner: no first-party S5 agent or legitimate parent identity-policy owner with returned organizational closure evidenced. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: GooseMode, permission manager, child role limits, working-directory checks, extension configuration, prompts and maintainers&#39; repository governance. - Closure path: tool approval or denial returns to the requesting coding turn; no ultimate identity/policy issue → legitimate authority decision → durable governing return loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-visible permissions and provider configuration do not grant authority over the system&#39;s defining purpose or arbitrate S3/S4 tensions. - Evidence: agent.rs, summon.rs, orchestrator.rs. - Basis: bounded review of identity, permission and session authority paths. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: maintainers may make strategic choices for the OSS project, but their external release organization is excluded from this local runtime boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/97bfad8a82a406f852f274e4e5a56f8681ad651c/assessments/ka/karmx.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 97bfad8a82a406f852f274e4e5a56f8681ad651c\">97bfad8 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-01T15:02:38+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/alessioricco/Certo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Certo</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-01\">2026-11-01</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/alessioricco/Certo/tree/d4b58e5113809a82f2fe6a42dbc76306663b4241\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision d4b58e5113809a82f2fe6a42dbc76306663b4241\">d4b58e5 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomous coding-task transformation through the assembled first-party execution pipeline. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown repositories, implementation failures and model/tool errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose and execute code/tool actions and corrective actions on received check feedback. - Decision owner: Certo&#39;s configured builder agent in an OpenHands conversation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: agent/tool wiring, workspace/project environment, persisted Conversation, task tracker and bounded retries. - Closure path: tool results and harness check/task-tracker follow-ups return into the builder conversation; final outcome is recorded. - Boundary reachability: runner streamtask builds the configured first-party Agent and invokes its SDK conversation with the requested workspace, task and tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the builder&#39;s model choice leaves execution/check machinery without discretionary implementation. - Evidence: src/certo/agent.py; src/certo/tools.py; src/certo/runner.py; README.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: SDK supplies underlying model/tool loop; only Certo&#39;s assembled operating mode is credited.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no distinct-S1 interference/coordination relation established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the function-specific disturbance described below is not regulated by a shipped organizational loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific decisive organizational right established. - Decision owner: not established; runtime mechanisms retain their narrower implementation role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the inspected task, lifecycle or configuration mechanisms below. - Closure path: no function-specific returned decision into subsequent regulation established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model stages take over the same coding task; independent writers produce audit probes in separate scratch areas. No concrete interacting operational S1 cells with a shipped disturbance-specific mutual coordination loop were established. - Evidence: src/certo/runner.py; src/certo/modelselection.py; src/certo/escalation.py; src/certo/independenttests.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Bounded to the pinned supported distribution; hypothetical downstream extensions are not assessed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no separate whole-current organizational regulatory decision loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the function-specific disturbance described below is not regulated by a shipped organizational loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific decisive organizational right established. - Decision owner: not established; runtime mechanisms retain their narrower implementation role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the inspected task, lifecycle or configuration mechanisms below. - Closure path: no function-specific returned decision into subsequent regulation established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: These paths enforce one coding task&#39;s completion/resource contract and fixed fallback rules. Generic task continuation/action approval does not establish whole-system current resource/commitment bargaining; no distinct function-specific autonomous or parent regulatory closure was found. - Evidence: src/certo/runner.py; src/certo/deadline.py; src/certo/costlimit.py; src/certo/modelselection.py; src/certo/escalation.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Bounded to the pinned supported distribution; hypothetical downstream extensions are not assessed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: complementary task-grounded challenge to builder/project-check claims in strict independent-tests mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a builder can pass its own checks while omitting requirements or sharing mistaken assumptions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently derive expected behavior from the original task/pre-build project, then challenge acceptance with protected test evidence. - Decision owner: separate test-writer agents supply the independent oracle judgment; deterministic run/vote/strict-verdict machinery transports it. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: scratch folders outside builder workspace, pre-build copy, editor-only conversations, test hashes, post-build execution/cleanup and majority vote. - Closure path: runner applies strict independent failures after ordinary checks/acceptance, changing verified into independentfailed and persisting the actual final verdict returned to the caller. - Boundary reachability: streamtask invokes writeindependenttests before build and applyindependenttests plus applystrictindependenttests afterward; local non-resume mode only. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the agent authors the independent expected-behavior tests; the harness&#39;s vote/parser does not own that judgment. Removing writer discretion leaves no independent oracle generation. - Evidence: src/certo/independenttests.py; src/certo/runner.py; src/certo/config.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: report-only mode alone is not credited as corrective closure. Strict failure prevents successful acceptance; it does not automatically repair the code. Test quality is not guaranteed and split/error/none do not downgrade. - Claim being audited: the builder&#39;s final project meets its task despite ordinary checks passing. - Ordinary reporting path: builder narrative, task completion and project verification verdict. - Complementary access path: independently specified hidden probes, authored from task/pre-build state and executed against finished artifacts. - Independence boundary: writers never see builder work; tests remain outside the project during build and are hash-checked before execution. - Who acts on findings: the first-party strict verdict path withholds verified acceptance and returns/persists independentfailed for caller control.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: operator-governed adaptation of the deployed model-capability repertoire for future task runs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: available/reachable provider models differ from the previously configured repertoire; future tasks need a chosen capability/cost strategy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: after discovery/probes, choose supported models and ratings, then select persistent manual/auto/escalate operating mode. - Decision owner: the legitimate local operator in certo init models; no autonomous S4 owner credited. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: reachable-model enumeration, tool-calling test, model/catalog/profile files, mode preview and user-settings persistence. - Closure path: only working candidates are written; returned operator choice writes models.yaml/catalog data and CERTOMODELSELECTION, consumed by subsequent task configuration and model ranking/ladder. - Boundary reachability: initmodels.main is shipped terminal CLI administration; its fetch/check/choose/write/preview path persists settings used by normal runner/modelselection. Not a research-only benchmark script. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the operator decides the future repertoire; deterministic discovery/probing and serialization implement that decision. Per-task fallback alone remains ordinary operation. - Evidence: src/certo/initmodels.py; src/certo/modelcatalog.py; src/certo/modelselection.py; src/certo/config.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: narrow parent capability-onboarding loop, not autonomous learning or continuous environmental strategy; user ratings may be subjective. Ordinary static YAML edits alone would not justify P. - External distinction: models installed on the local Ollama server or available through configured providers, plus actual tool-calling viability. - Future / prospective distinction: which capability/cost repertoire and model-selection strategy will be available for future coding tasks. - Adaptation option generated: working candidate sets with known/user-provided ratings and previewed manual, auto-model or escalation choices. - Path back into current capability / S3: operator activation writes model/setting files; subsequent loadconfig/catalog selection changes the actual builder model repertoire. No separate positive S3 is inferred. - Parent adaptation closure: discovered/tested options → operator choice → persisted active model set/mode → subsequent task execution under that capability choice.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity/ultimate-policy authority-return path established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the function-specific disturbance described below is not regulated by a shipped organizational loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific decisive organizational right established. - Decision owner: not established; runtime mechanisms retain their narrower implementation role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the inspected task, lifecycle or configuration mechanisms below. - Closure path: no function-specific returned decision into subsequent regulation established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: These paths set coding-task constraints, approve actions or adapt the model repertoire. No identity/ultimate-policy dispute is routed to a legitimate ultimate authority and returned as a resolved governing policy. Contributor governance is outside the product boundary. - Evidence: src/certo/agent.py; src/certo/config.py; src/certo/runner.py; src/certo/initmodels.py; src/certo/acceptance.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Bounded to the pinned supported distribution; hypothetical downstream extensions are not assessed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ff2f9d91a5686cb47d33d8115656acfe5eba5780/assessments/ce/certo.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ff2f9d91a5686cb47d33d8115656acfe5eba5780\">ff2f9d9 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-17T19:38:49+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Gen-Verse/ScienceBuddy\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ScienceBuddy / Simple-SciBuddy</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-17\">2026-10-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Gen-Verse/ScienceBuddy/tree/53177995fd549068cbe25282273bed771e843f50\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 53177995fd549068cbe25282273bed771e843f50\">5317799 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: A scientific task model solves individual tasks through first-party program-directed generation, tool execution and submission cycles. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Varied scientific user questions, reasoning uncertainty, tool/answer format failures, changing observations and additional simulated researcher feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The task model chooses reasoning/output and possible tool calls under a runnable Python program&#39;s control, responds to actual observations and submits a scientific answer. A replacement candidate can change harness call/order/context strategies. - Decision owner: First-party run(task, api) program plus connected model-inference runtime; the scientific task&#39;s model owns content decisions, and frozen code owns interaction/action feedback. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: API.generate/execute/submit, bounded broker messages, isolated controller sandbox, input/token limits, recorded trajectories and task/verifier datasets. - Closure path: scientific question → harness/model action → actual execution/tool output → model continuation and submitted answer → recorded result/reply, optionally next conversation turn. - Boundary reachability: coevolve.phase.batch calls harness.broker.runepisode with first-party scientific run(task,api); the broker executes the installed Python harness controller, mediates actual model generation/execution/submission and saves episode events. Same path feeds inner and validation tasks. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The task model and harness generate contingent actions and explanations from actual task/tool feedback, not a prewritten fixed answer table. - Evidence: src/simplescibuddy/harness/scientific.py; src/simplescibuddy/harness/broker.py; src/simplescibuddy/harness/controller.py; src/simplescibuddy/coevolve/phase.py. - Basis: Executable first-party task-agent/model-feedback path. - Confidence: High source path; no model-produced empirical result validated in this review. - Caveats: RL training and model weights are external dependencies; hosted scientific workspace is excluded.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No source-backed inter-S1 mutual disturbance or interference-regulation feedback established beyond shared batch throughput caps. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Different candidate and parent evaluations simultaneously compete for inference throughput, but code does not demonstrate a documented resource collision/oscillation remedied by adaptive S2 balancing. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A configured semaphore serializes/caps concurrent task batches; no separate decision adapts conflicting S1 operating mandates or co-activity after observing a disturbance. - Decision owner: Deterministic batch executor and configured worker concurrency limit. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: One shared validation semaphore across parent/candidate evaluations, task-specific isolated runtime and result recording. - Closure path: Task batch → semaphore admission → episode completion frees token. This is general capacity management, not a demonstrated conflict signal → inter-S1 correction → feedback to changed sibling operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Pure concurrency limiting without observed inter-operational conflict and responsive attenuation must not be inflated into autonomous or constructor-owned S2. - Evidence: src/simplescibuddy/coevolve/phase.py; src/simplescibuddy/coevolve/selection.py; src/simplescibuddy/harness/broker.py. - Basis: Negative bounded causal search of shared execution and parallel candidate evaluation. - Confidence: Medium; improvement would need documented clash between distinct S1 activity lanes and a meaningful active attenuation return. - Caveats: The semaphore is a real first-party concurrency guard; exclusion of S2 does not deny resource protection.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: Regulate present multi-episode experiment phase and commitments: selected harness/model, Train/Val/Test boundaries, current candidate submissions/validation tasks, task execution budget, training handoff and fail-closed state. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unsupported Python candidate programs, incomplete or incomparable validation coverage, evidence leakage into proposals, failed workers, changed frozen data and unstable training/assessment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: First-party coevolve.loop.run schedules current harness learning and RL model updates, freezes dataset identities, advances Train cursor, calls candidate selector, triggers or halts training based on selected improvement and checks worker result before updating current model/harness. - Decision owner: Deterministic first-party coevolution/controller code and static experimental budget/config. The auxiliary model proposes and task model operates; neither owns whole-experiment now-control. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Frozen identities, Train cursor, manifests, complete heldout Val match, preflight, selection winner and validation trace, request/result integrity and stop conditions. - Closure path: Current episodes, candidate readiness and validation outcomes → source controller chooses reject/adopt/stop/train/new model → changes active harness/model and next execution commitments → writes persisted phase history and repeats. - Whole-system current view: Parent and candidate harness identities, current task-model checkpoint, frozen train/val/test source, worker status, phase counters and comparative evaluation results for the whole experiment. - Current-control decision scope: Govern current allocation to scientific task batches, compared candidate evaluations, training worker, later phase start and hard stop, not just an individual scientific task. - Boundary reachability: coevolve.loop.run invokes harnessevolve and reads phasesummary[&quot;improved&quot;] before publishing/claiming training requests; selectcandidates decides adoption from complete Val results, and validateresult supplies next model to the next harness phase. This is the runnable experiment entry, not an offline report. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Selection/admission/train handoff and return are explicit first-party rules, not an autonomous policy model choosing all current resource mandates; S3=C. - Evidence: src/simplescibuddy/coevolve/loop.py; src/simplescibuddy/coevolve/phase.py; src/simplescibuddy/coevolve/selection.py; src/simplescibuddy/coevolve/protocol.py. - Basis: Installed full-current experiment and verified phase transitions. - Confidence: High for deterministic current control, bounded to experiment recursion. - Caveats: No enterprise lab portfolio governance inferred; SkyRL provides computation inside a structured worker contract.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: Independently check proposed candidate claims of improved scientific task performance against privately retained ground-truth evidence, and return veto/admission to current control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A model-proposed harness change can hallucinate improvement, accidentally overfit public examples, fail to submit valid answers or contaminate proposed evidence with Val/Test labels. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Host private-reference verifier grades actual completed Val episodes, requires complete same-task coverage and zero candidate execution errors; candidate replaces parent only on strictly higher correct-task count. No model critic owns that alternative correctness decision. - Decision owner: First-party deterministic held-out verifier and selection mechanism, with private reference answers withheld from the candidate/proposer; choice is constructor-owned. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Data verifier, frozen Val task IDs, task-answer collection from episodes, parent/candidate same-model comparison, valid program preflight, private ground-truth isolation. - Closure path: Model-improver proposed harness improvement → actual unseen held-out Val task rollout and host private-reference scoring → controller rejects/accepts replacement → selected/rejected harness changes future S1 operation and next evolution round. - Claim being audited: Whether a proposed Python harness program is scientifically valid operationally and actually improves scored task success relative to current parent. - Ordinary reporting path: Improver propose with hypothesis and public execution feedback; candidate program runs episodes and submits answers. - Complementary access path: Independent private reference labels and actual held-out Val execution paths are hidden from proposal inputs; structured host scoring verifies answers under matched tasks/model and full coverage. - Independence boundary: Model improver cannot read Val/Test oracle labels or score before proposals freeze; controller owns the verifier and may share code authorship but not the model&#39;s candidate claims. This is a constructor/host-scored audit, not a separate autonomous model judge. - Who acts on findings: selectcandidates computes exact parent/candidate accuracy and checks zero errors; chooses winner or retains parent. The selected source file and its validation receipt feed next harness/model phase through harnessevolve/coevolve.loop. - Boundary reachability: harnessevolve calls selectcandidates, which runs every candidate and parent on the Val panel and only writes status: applied for strict improvements; the loop uses selected harness file for later Train/RL phases. Verified on source, not inference speculation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A separate private oracle determines whether current change claims hold, but comparison/admission logic is deterministic, so S3=C rather than A. Optional auxiliary PRM feedback interpretation is hypothesis generation, not promoted as an independent auditor. - Evidence: src/simplescibuddy/coevolve/selection.py; src/simplescibuddy/data/verifier.py; src/simplescibuddy/coevolve/phase.py; src/simplescibuddy/coevolve/evidence.py. - Basis: First-party source-attested independent verifier and binding quality-admission path. - Confidence: Medium-high for held-out auditable comparison; this is not a human external independent review. - Caveats: Same dataset owner/controller may govern protocols; performance verification is not proof of every scientific intermediate step. Full Val coverage does not guarantee deployment generalization.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: Prospective improve the operational harness&#39;s future scientific-task capacity by diagnosing externally encountered task environment, proposing alternative complete executable harness designs, testing their future performance and returning an adopted change into actual ongoing S3/S1 control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Poor scientific task success across diverse external Train scenarios, recurrent tool errors, context clipping, unreliable reasoning, shifting effectiveness of model/harness combinations after RL updates. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Independent auxiliary model Improver selects evidence-grounded hypotheses and full replacement Python harness programs; source controller tests alternatives on heldout Val and adopts only validated improvements, then uses selected program in next task-model and evolution stages. - Decision owner: First-party configured auxiliary model owns new harness idea/program content and selects prospective modifications; deterministic S3 selection holds performance/admission rights. No external hosted ScienceBuddy product decisions imported. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Public episode evidence and validated feedback, explicit program schema, bounded read/propose/skip, program AST safety gate, Train preflight, matched Val selection and actual file replacement, alternating GRPO model update. - Closure path: Task-environment episodes/feedback and tool failure → auxiliary-model future harness hypotheses/complete Python programs → independent Val testing → S3 select/adopt → actual new run(task, api) program guides future task episodes / model training → new model checkpoint returns for next adaptation phase. - External distinction: The external Train task distribution and observed researcher/tool errors or feedback vary beyond the currently executing task and create empirically distinct demands on the agent harness, from task episode evidence rather than maintainer memory. - Future / prospective distinction: Candidate changes target the performance of subsequent heldout Val and future Train/Test tasks after the present task episode; the next model/harness cycle is deliberately separate in time from the observed failures. - Adaptation option generated: The auxiliary model creates executable alternative complete Python run(task, api) harness programs that change prompt construction, tool orchestration, context management and submission handling; independent Val comparison selects or rejects each candidate. - Path back into current capability / S3: Host S3 selection writes the winner as the active harness file, uses it for subsequent episodes and RL model training, and feeds the exported model checkpoint plus harness to the next coevolution phase, closing the option-to-operation return. - External and prospective orientation: The future task distribution and observed researcher/tool environment provide performance pressures; the auxiliary model develops design alternatives to change the agent&#39;s operating harness for tasks beyond current episode, rather than only answering the current scientific question. - Adaptation options: Full Python run replacements modify prompt organization, parsing, tool use, context/memory and control flow; a candidate can be rejected with history or be adopted under strictly better Val accuracy. - Two-way return with current control: Current episode failures and validation feedback supply improver inputs; the S3 experiment controller accepts a verified candidate, replaces active harness, trains/updates model and schedules the next current task batch with the changed capability. Future cycle feedback re-enters improver. - Boundary reachability: coevolve.phase.harnessevolve gathers source-safe public evidence and calls coevolve.program.propose; selection.selectcandidates can select a different actual harness.py file; coevolve.loop.run receives returned harness and trains/starts subsequent rounds with it. This is executable first-party adaptation in configured evolution mode, not passive documentation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The auxiliary model autonomously authors new harness program/hypothesis options based on evidence; constructor selection supplies safety/performance gating. Thus adaptive design generation is A within strict model+host composite, not merely tool/config hot reload. - Evidence: src/simplescibuddy/coevolve/program.py; src/simplescibuddy/coevolve/evidence.py; src/simplescibuddy/coevolve/improver.py; src/simplescibuddy/coevolve/selection.py; src/simplescibuddy/coevolve/loop.py. - Basis: Exact first-party source of longitudinal harness proposals, empirical selection and return-to-operation path. - Confidence: High that mechanism is implemented; no observed percentage improvement or completed multi-round GPU training asserted. - Caveats: S4 applies to configured evolution mode, not every isolated task run. Model training stack and third-party CUDA packages are dependencies, and positive score is not a claim of scientific truth outside frozen tasks.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No installed ultimate ethos/identity/policy dispute resolution for the scientific agent organization; predefined scientific task and improvement goals are inherited rather than governed. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Public/private dataset confidentiality, artifact tampering, invalid program execution, training protocol mismatches and runtime permissions are handled by fixed rules. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Experiment controller enforces frozen task split, code safety/preflight, model identity checks and strict Val improvement; it does not decide whether maximizing a frozen verifier&#39;s task accuracy is the legitimate ultimate purpose or negotiate value conflicts with a parent institution. - Decision owner: Maintainer / human experiment designer defines science goal and policy/config; first-party loop executes and reports under those constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Immutable protocol manifests, private oracle data, AST program validation, task-content redaction, reproducible Train cursor, checkpoint hashes and stop reasons. - Closure path: Fixed experiment rules → operational acceptance/rejection. No independent legitimate-policy conflict → highest-level purpose choice → binding return to S1–S4. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Self-improving harness code does not imply self-governing mission/ethos; neither static experiment config nor signed checkpoints supply policy S5. - Evidence: src/simplescibuddy/coevolve/loop.py; src/simplescibuddy/coevolve/protocol.py; src/simplescibuddy/coevolve/program.py; configs/defaults.json. - Basis: Bounded negative source assessment of top-level objective and legitimacy ownership. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: External maintainers or research institutions may be ultimate-policy owners beyond this open-source runtime; not automatically scored as parent S5.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/6ee3760070fd875c36798fef7b94cd326f1e4789/assessments/sciencebuddy-simple-scibuddy.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 6ee3760070fd875c36798fef7b94cd326f1e4789\">6ee3760 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-27T12:58:44Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Epsirom/braid\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Braid</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-27\">2026-10-27</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Epsirom/braid/tree/993b3a61941401695698eff8ccc64d4da61c350a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 993b3a61941401695698eff8ccc64d4da61c350a\">993b3a6 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: produce task results and effect selected code integration through isolated model invocations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing code inputs, Git errors, tool failures and contradictory source results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select task actions/decision choices and merge Git operations/source dispositions after observations. - Decision owner: model-backed node agents; specifically the bundled merge/integrate agent and first-party installed worker loops. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: graph admission, workspace capabilities, local Git command allowlist, checkpoints, schemas, time/token limits and finish validation. - Closure path: merge runner executes model-selected Git calls, returns their output and errors into subsequent model turns, then records source dispositions and final output. - Boundary reachability: createOpenAICompatibleRunner is a shipped adapter with its own merge tool loop; first-party Pi/DSH runners additionally wire richer node tools. No custom runner is needed to demonstrate this narrow S1. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: core does not choose a merge strategy or source acceptance; the model does. Text-only OpenAI worker replies alone are not used as proof of a general action loop. - Evidence: bundled actor/tool setup; call/tool/observation loop; explicit source decisions; first-party worker factory. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: OpenAI execute nodes have no general filesystem/shell tools. Platform adapters differ in capabilities; no empirical task-quality claim is made.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: reconcile conflicting concurrent code contributions without overwriting sibling/caller state. - Disturbance / variety regulated: parallel agents produce diverging edits and integration can collide with pre-existing or concurrent caller changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect source checkpoints/dirty status, choose which changes to integrate/discard/archive and choose/repair the Git integration method. - Decision owner: merge/integrate model agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fresh worktrees, immutable predecessor checkpoints, invocation-specific Git operations, source dirty preview, integration serialization and finishmerge conflict checks. - Closure path: Git conflicts/errors return to the model; selected source dispositions and reconciled checkpoint become the inputs for subsequent workers or the user&#39;s source checkout. - Boundary reachability: explicit merge/integrate node types invoke the bundled agent loop and core beginMerge/finish path. The path handles actual conflicting code states, not generic graph routing. - Distinct S1 units: independent worker node agents and the model-backed reconciliation unit consuming their outputs. - Inter-S1 disturbance: incompatible sibling edits or accidental replay/overwrite of the invoking user&#39;s existing edits. - Attenuating coordination relation: isolate contributions, then perform agent-selected explicit reconciliation with one accounted disposition per source. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: downstream work uses the reconciled checkpoint; failed/unresolved integration blocks a successful finish and preserves recovery evidence. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the agent explicitly handles code collision and source-selection variety across independently produced artifacts, beyond waiting for graph edges. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: core validates/enforces boundaries; it explicitly does not apply or select changes on behalf of the model. - Evidence: coordination contract; collision/source decision tools; feedback loop; merge completion guards. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: In-process integration serialization does not coordinate arbitrary external processes; source changes can leave partial edits/conflicts and a backup.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C(P) - Function: hold, revise and resume whole-job commitments in response to current work findings and constrained execution resources. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a worker discovers redundant/incorrect planned work, live work must be stopped or redirected, or graph/time/token limits are reached. - Decisive decision or feedback right: graph owner decides whether to alter nodes/edges/limits or continue/cancel affected executions. - Decision owner: base mode exposes this right to a consumer-composed autonomous graph owner; parent mode supplies a human operator through the first-party panels. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: whole-job snapshots, execution/revision identities, replan holding and reminders, update/resume/limit API, suspension checkpoints and panel controls. - Closure path: a worker&#39;s replan request holds successors; owner inspection and revision-checked update/resume changes subsequent scheduling. Human pause/hold/resume/cancel similarly returns directly into the live job. - Boundary reachability: specific requestReplan and control methods are intentionally exposed by core; installed adapters notify a host primary agent. The human live panels are first-party operational surfaces. The external primary agent&#39;s own autonomy is not credited as Braid-owned A. - Whole-system current view: job snapshot includes all node/execution states, paused IDs, current graph revision, results and finite limits. - Current-control decision scope: job-wide current commitments, redundant work, execution admission and resource/time allowance. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: workers cannot rewrite the graph themselves. Braid provides the control-specific feedback/right but no autonomous whole-graph owner in its standalone bundled runner; the external application/host must compose that actor. Human panel intervention has its own complete parent return. - Evidence: finding-specific control primitive; hold/notification wiring; whole-job control contract; parent notification; human current intervention; human return. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: C is for a function-specific control path, not arbitrary framework expressiveness. The P mode is local graph supervision, not ultimate policy or simultaneous agent/human ownership.\">C(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no separate complementary audit of Braid operational reporting beyond its ordinary work/review paths established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: false implementation or validation claims are relevant, but available review is task production/QA. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no independently controlled complementary audit of the runtime&#39;s ordinary reports supplied. - Decision owner: none established for S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: read-only reviewers with raw files/diffs, check decision node, checkpoints/activity logs and fake-provider/native tests. - Closure path: the review command sends findings through its prescribed check→fix→re-review→apply production cycle. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: distinct fresh reviewer contexts and raw evidence are useful, but mandatory QA in that production graph is not automatically S3. - Evidence: reviewer purpose and evidence boundary; ordinary review/fix decision cycle; graph integration checks. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Source-relative finding; no empirical model-quality claim.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no external-and-prospective capability-adaptation loop established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future provider/tool/environment changes would require adaptation outside the current job. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no future-option/adoption process with a return to present capability supplied. - Decision owner: none established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: live graph edits, requestreplan, cyclic refinement, model overrides and prompt templates. - Closure path: findings alter the present job&#39;s plan or rerun current work, rather than develop external/future adaptation options for the harness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: replanning the current task and model selection are not S4 by themselves. - Evidence: present-work replan semantics; current graph scheduling/cycles; task/model parameters. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source-relative finding; no empirical model-quality claim.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity/ultimate-policy issue and legitimate authoritative-return loop established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ultimate purpose/delegation conflicts are not a supplied job workflow. - Decisive decision or feedback right: graph goals, workspace capabilities and user pauses are task/control decisions, not ultimate-policy settlement. - Decision owner: none established for S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: trusted adapter, graph schema, read/write capability flags, command allowlist and user-versus-agent control attribution. - Closure path: settings and graph actions constrain task execution; no identity-level proposal reaching ultimate authority and returning as policy is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: calling the task submitter an owner or allowing it to pause nodes does not establish S5. - Evidence: adapter trust boundary; scoped merge capabilities; ordinary owner control. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source-relative finding; no empirical model-quality claim.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/566e70e0bfffa79cab25cd03e4786968bf00bb7a/assessments/br/braid.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 566e70e0bfffa79cab25cd03e4786968bf00bb7a\">566e70e &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-05T21:34:22+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/woolcoxm/MyHarness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MyHarness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-05\">2026-10-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/woolcoxm/MyHarness/tree/801082b90e182badb93ad3aca1946e4ff9ccfae6\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 801082b90e182badb93ad3aca1946e4ff9ccfae6\">801082b &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously perform coding work through model-selected repository/tool actions and iterative feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar codebases, file state, command/build/test failures, tool/provider errors, incomplete requirements, context pressure and long-running goal completion uncertainty. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next inspection/edit/command/subagent action, revise work from returned evidence, and decide when to claim task completion. - Decision owner: the active model-backed MyHarness coding agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent::runturn, coding tools, steering, verify hooks, autonomous continuation, permissions/sandboxing, sessions, MCP/LSP bridges, skills and subagent tool. - Closure path: task/context → model chooses action/tool → concrete result or subagent report returns → model revises subsequent work → repeat until completion, interruption or bounded stop. - Boundary reachability: TUI, headless and autonomous entry points instantiate the same first-party agent/tool loop directly. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the model actor, the runtime retains tools, validators and persistence but loses the open-ended decisions that transform evidence into coding actions. - Evidence: README; src/agent/mod.rs; src/main.rs; src/tools/mod.rs; src/tools/task.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external providers supply inference but not first-party organizational ownership.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no distinct first-party inter-S1 coordination loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple subagents can run in parallel and coder subagents may share the workspace, but no shipped mechanism specifically detects and attenuates peer interference among them. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no S2-specific conflict/oscillation decision was found. - Decision owner: not established at S2 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: subagent tool restriction, independent contexts, background-task handles, shared cancellation and parent polling. - Closure path: parent delegation/report collection sequences or parallelizes work but does not close a peer-interference attenuation loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: generic delegation and concurrency are not S2 without a concrete disturbance-specific coordination relation. - Evidence: src/tools/task.rs; src/agent/mod.rs; README. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: parallel coder subagents may themselves create workspace contention; the absence of a dedicated coordination path is why this is not credited positively.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no distinct whole-system current-control function is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: turn limits, budgets, background tasks, todo state, permissions and autonomous completion checks regulate one focal coding run and its delegated helpers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no separate authority holds a whole-system current view and substantively manages a portfolio of current S1 commitments/resources/priorities. - Decision owner: not established at S3 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: autonomous budgets, turn caps, todo state, background-task registry, steering, permissions and session state. - Closure path: these mechanisms constrain or inform the same operational loop rather than forming a separate whole-current management loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the parent agent delegates and consumes reports as part of S1 task execution; no distinct metasystemic current-control owner is exposed. - Evidence: src/main.rs; src/agent/mod.rs; src/tools/task.rs; src/tools/todo.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: subagent dispatch alone is insufficient for S3 without a whole-system current view and substantive current-control scope.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge implementation/correctness claims by direct fresh-context review or testing and return findings to the producing parent agent. - Disturbance / variety regulated: defects, incorrect assumptions, security problems and unverified completion claims that may survive the producer&#39;s own reasoning or self-check. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the reviewer/tester subagent decides which directly observed repository/test evidence constitutes a material finding or failure and reports that judgment to the parent. - Decision owner: the separate model-backed subagent instantiated through the first-party task tool. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fresh AgentState, isolated model context, role-specific tool subsets, documented reviewer/tester task modes, direct workspace/search/read/bash access and final-report return. - Closure path: parent/producer changes or proposes workspace state → parent dispatches reviewer/tester subagent → child directly inspects repository and/or executes verification → child report returns as tool output → parent changes later coding/repair action based on findings. - Boundary reachability: task is a shipped first-party tool and explicitly documents code-review/testing uses; no external reviewer product is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the child model leaves role/tool plumbing but removes the independent semantic audit judgment over inspected evidence. - Evidence: README; src/tools/task.rs; src/agent/mod.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: dispatch is chosen by the parent and review is not mandatory for every task; the positive state concerns the supported first-party complementary-audit mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no externally and prospectively oriented adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: zero-mem retrieves prior-session evidence, skills provide reusable instructions, web/MCP/LSP tools expose external information and session replay restores past state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party actor generates and installs future-oriented harness capability adaptations from environmental change. - Decision owner: not established at S4 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: zero-mem BM25/entity retrieval, static skills, session replay, web tools, MCP/LSP integrations and context compaction. - Closure path: remembered/external evidence feeds current task execution; it does not close an outside-and-then adaptation cycle back into durable harness capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: persistence, retrieval and external research are insufficient for S4 without prospective distinction, adaptation-option generation and capability return. - Evidence: README; src/zeromem.rs; src/skills.rs; src/session.rs; src/mcp.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: users can manually create skills or reconfigure providers, but generic extensibility is not autonomous organizational adaptation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity- or ultimate-policy-level closure is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: permission modes, deny/allow rules, approval prompts, sandbox boundaries, hooks, workspace scoping and authentication constrain ordinary operation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no identity/ultimate-policy issue is routed to a legitimate ultimate authority and returned as a durable system-level policy decision. - Decision owner: not established at S5 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permission engine, plan/ask/auto-edit/yolo modes, sandboxing, hooks, auth, workspace/write guards and configured tool rules. - Closure path: operator configuration and approvals affect current actions but do not close identity-level policy questions for the harness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: static/configurable safety and access constraints are enforcement mechanisms, not S5. - Evidence: README; src/perms.rs; src/sandbox.rs; src/hooks.rs; src/auth.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: maintainers/operators retain authority outside a run, but development/deployment governance is adjacent to the assessed operating boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/cdfb1b9d4f2e1d13019b2fc9aaa584823469e59e/assessments/myharness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit cdfb1b9d4f2e1d13019b2fc9aaa584823469e59e\">cdfb1b9 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-03T03:50:08+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/amuluze/axiom-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Axiom</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-03\">2026-10-03</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/amuluze/axiom-agent/tree/da7b98b739a824713d230b2635edb56868553696\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision da7b98b739a824713d230b2635edb56868553696\">da7b98b &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform environment-facing engineering work by interpreting the objective, inspecting artifacts, selecting coding/design/deployment tools, proposing or applying authorized changes, observing results and iterating toward completion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous project structure and code, incomplete task information, tool/provider failures, approval outcomes, changing workspace state, tests/commands, design/browser/remote evidence, context pressure and restart/recovery conditions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next task-specific engineering action/tool, which evidence to gather or state to change, and when the requested operation is complete. - Decision owner: the main model-backed Axiom agent operating through the first-party model/tool runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool registry/discovery; workspace authorization and approval leases; sandbox and redaction; durable session/recovery journal; context compaction; queues; runtime budgets; provider transport; project docs/skills; checkpoints and branch/retry support. - Closure path: user objective/current session context → model selects action/tool → Axiom enforcement and tool execution against the environment → result/error persisted and returned → next model turn revises action or completes. - Boundary reachability: the normal production desktop runtime directly instantiates the first-party AgentSession/AgentHarness with the product tool runtime; no downstream application author must compose a separate agent loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: if the main model actor is removed while deterministic tools, approvals, persistence and recovery remain, the open-ended engineering choice of what to inspect/change/do next disappears. - Evidence: README.md; runAgentLoop.ts; AgentSession.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: side-effecting tools can require user approval, but approval gates the main agent&#39;s proposed action rather than supplying the engineering judgment itself.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party inter-S1 coordination function is established at the assessed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no concrete collision, interference or oscillation among distinct production S1 units is present in the standard organization. Reviewer/explore children are scoped read-only subordinate roles invoked synchronously for evidence/audit rather than peer workers mutating a shared operational domain. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established at S2 scope. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: child scope isolation; read-only child tool set; parent/child budgets; sequential child tool execution; message queues; durable mutation/session journals; workspace authorization. - Closure path: not applicable; no distinct production-S1 interference → attenuation → changed later S1 behavior relation was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: SubAgent plurality does not itself establish S2. The frozen runtime supplies reviewer/explorer support units, but no shared-write peer population or concrete inter-operational conflict requiring coordination. - Evidence: SubAgentRuntime.ts; contracts.ts; createToolRegistry.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: separate child model sessions are real actors, but their read-only reviewer/explorer roles do not turn them into mutually interfering production S1s.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no separate whole-system current-control function over a population of operational commitments/resources is established at the assessed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: run budgets, queue state, approvals, runtime updates, recovery, child delegation limits and lifecycle hooks regulate one main engineering session and its support/audit calls rather than a whole organization of production S1 units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established at S3 scope. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentSession/AgentHarness lifecycle; message queues; mutation journal; turn save points; runtime hooks; subagent parent-run ledger; abort/deadline controls; approval coordinator; recovery/checkpoints. - Closure path: these paths constrain, recover or support the focal operating session. No actor with a whole-system view chooses or revises shared production commitments, priorities, resource allocations or cross-S1 interventions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the main agent can choose to delegate a reviewer/explorer, but managing support calls inside its own task is ordinary S1 work, not evidence of S3 over distinct operational units. - Evidence: AgentSession.ts; AgentHarness.ts; contracts.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: extensive runtime control and recovery should not be promoted to S3 merely because they supervise execution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently review specifications/plans/code changes through a separate read-only model session, judge them against direct project evidence and return pass/fail findings into the parent agent&#39;s corrective workflow. - Disturbance / variety regulated: specification/plan defects, implementation bugs, security regressions, inadequate tests, contract violations and cases where the implementer&#39;s ordinary self-assessment would otherwise allow incorrect work to finish. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the child reviewer independently decides a structured pass/fail audit verdict and prioritizes concrete path:line findings from its own scoped read evidence; the parent model receives that verdict as a tool result and owns the subsequent correction/re-review response. - Decision owner: a separate model-backed Axiom reviewer child for the substantive audit judgment. Deterministic verdict parsing and notices only expose/enforce the child&#39;s conclusion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: default subagent:review capability; discoveragenttools; review/inspect/examine tool contracts; separate SubAgent child session; scoped read-only environment; reviewer-specific prompt; optional actual diff supplied by the parent; child/parent budgets; verdict parser and fail notice. - Closure path: main agent implements/specifies/plans → autonomously discovers and invokes reviewer → separate child reads scoped operational artifacts (and code diff when supplied) → child emits pass/fail + findings → first-party tool parses and returns verdict to parent → fail notice and canonical SDD rules direct parent back to repair → parent can change the work and re-run review before finish. - Boundary reachability: production desktop policy grants subagent:review by default, product startup creates and injects the first-party SubAgentRuntime, the review tools are registered in the ordinary tool table, and always-active discoveragenttools lets the main model activate them without developer composition or user approval. - Claim being audited: that the current Task Spec/Plan/implementation is sufficiently correct, safe, tested and contract-compatible to advance or finish. - Ordinary reporting path: the main engineering agent&#39;s own plan/implementation and ordinary tool/test results inside its working session. - Complementary access path: a separately instantiated child model session with a reviewer-specific system prompt and scoped read-only access re-reads the current workspace independently; for code review it can additionally receive the parent-collected Git diff as authoritative change evidence. - Independence boundary: the reviewer has separate child session identity/context, cannot mutate the workspace or inherit the parent&#39;s conversation as an ordinary continuation, and forms its own pass/fail judgment from direct read evidence. It shares the configured model/transport family unless the operator changes that configuration, but the organizational judgment path is a distinct model invocation/session rather than the implementer&#39;s in-band self-report. - Who acts on findings: the main Axiom agent receives the reviewer result. On fail, the tool result explicitly instructs it to return to the previous stage, fix findings and re-delegate review; the standard SDD system prompt independently encodes fail → repair → re-review before finish. A user can explicitly accept risk and override the soft gate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the reviewer model while retaining the deterministic verdict parser, budgets and fail-message machinery removes the substantive independent defect judgment. The parser cannot manufacture a pass/fail audit finding on its own. - Evidence: runtimePolicy.ts; agentStore.ts; SubAgentRuntime.ts; createReviewerSubAgentTool.ts; buildReviewPrompt.ts; systemPromptSections.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the corrective gate is model-visible and workflow-enforced rather than a deterministic hard block; users can explicitly accept risk. That does not remove the autonomous complementary audit judgment or its normal corrective return path.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material external-and-prospective organizational adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: project docs/skills, provider/runtime settings, context compaction, session recovery and tool discovery alter current execution context/capability exposure but do not sense future environmental change and autonomously choose a persistent capability/strategy adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established at S4 scope. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: project document scanning/reload; project skill inventory/load; runtime dependency refresh; provider/model settings; context manager; durable session state and recovery. - Closure path: changed project files/configuration can be rescanned into later current prompts, but no first-party prospective scan → adaptation option generation → selected persistent organizational capability change → operational return loop is present. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: generic ability to write project files plus later reload does not establish a function-specific adaptation decision. The reload machinery consumes externally/currently changed artifacts rather than deciding a future capability change itself. - Evidence: projectDocs.ts; loadProjectSkills.ts; projectDocsRefresh.ts; AgentSession.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: durable reloadable project artifacts can influence later behavior, but persistence/reload alone is not S4 without a prospective adaptation function and decisive adaptation right.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material runtime identity or ultimate-policy closure is established at the assessed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: workspace authorization, approval modes, capability policy, prompts, project docs and user decisions constrain work, but they do not form a first-party identity/ultimate-policy adjudication loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established at S5 scope. - Decision owner: not established within the Axiom runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: production runtime policy; workspace authorization; approval coordinator/leases; system prompt; user/project documentation; settings; sandbox/network/security enforcement. - Closure path: policy/configuration is supplied or changed externally and enforced/loaded into operation. No identity/ultimate-policy issue is routed to a legitimate first-party S5 authority, resolved there and returned as an authoritative organization-wide policy change. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the main/reviewer agents operate under the supplied capability/security/project boundaries but do not possess authority to redefine Axiom&#39;s purpose, identity or ultimate policy. - Evidence: runtimePolicy.ts; ApprovalCoordinator.ts; systemPromptSections.ts; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: user approval can override a soft review gate and project owners can author durable guidance. Generic approval/configuration is not promoted to S5 without an identity/ultimate-policy function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/70175cff60657032ff976a1b5802131728209974/assessments/axiom.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 70175cff60657032ff976a1b5802131728209974\">70175cf &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-24T15:55:08+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/hasitpbhatt/codewhip\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CodeWhip</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-24\">2026-10-24</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/hasitpbhatt/codewhip/tree/be51da68db85096e9fa0455777e3ed89985df756\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision be51da68db85096e9fa0455777e3ed89985df756\">be51da6 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: transform coding objectives into repository inspection, edits, commands and validated responses through an agent-controlled model/tool feedback loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: varying source files, implementation alternatives, failed commands, provider/tool errors, returned observations and task-specific user needs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select and revise task-specific tool calls, code changes and next actions from observations. - Decision owner: the main model-backed CodeWhip Agent; optional child investigators separately choose their read/search steps. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CLI and SDK adapters, tool registry, path jail, policy ladder, denied action list, token/cost caps, checkpoint/rollback, sessions, hooks, outcomes and hash-chain. - Closure path: user prompt and source context → model-selected tool call → permission-gated tool execution or refusal → tool result entered into model transcript → subsequent revised action or final answer. - Boundary reachability: src/index.ts invokes the native agentLoop from src/loop.ts; shipped read/edit/write/bash/search tools are wired into the normal local run, not merely example scripts. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model leaves rules and deterministic tool executors, but no open-ended choice of coding modifications or revision from feedback; tool policy only constrains that choice. - Evidence: src/loop.ts, src/index.ts, tools. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: model-provider quality and actual coding effectiveness are not tested; human approvals and deterministic stop rules limit reachable actions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate shared token-budget exhaustion and parent-convergence starvation among independently running delegated S1 investigators in the supported concurrent fan-out mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: up to four simultaneous read-only child model loops can consume the same finite parent token allowance and prevent sibling work or the parent&#39;s final result. - Distinct S1 units: concurrently running delegated child model/tool loops, each with its own task, transcript, model decisions, timeout and returned output; parent coding loop is another operational actor. - Inter-S1 disturbance: unbounded sibling token consumption would deplete the parent quota needed for other children and the parent convergence step. - Attenuating coordination relation: delegatemany invokes allocateChildBudgets, divides the available budget into child quotas and reserves a separate coordination share, then passes enforceable ceilings to individual child loops. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: a child&#39;s token ceiling stops or bounds its continuation; completed/failed child reports and metered usage return to the parent model, which continues with that constrained result rather than unknowingly spending the entire shared budget. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the mechanism is explicitly tied to shared resource interference among simultaneous operational children, not merely their order or message transport. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide the concrete shared-budget attenuation policy (85% child pool, 15% parent reserve, equal child shares); the shipped code enforces the selected policy and reports outcomes. - Decision owner: CodeWhip developers/configured deterministic allocator for the budget-split policy; no first-party autonomous S2 actor selects or revises the quota policy from conflict feedback. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Promise.all child scheduling, tokenBudget caps in child agentLoop, per-child deadlines, usage folding, ordered report collection and the parent&#39;s model-selected delegatemany task set. - Closure path: finite parent budget and multiple children → first-party allocation → enforced per-child ceilings → bounded child work and result/usage feedback → parent continues with reserved capacity. - Boundary reachability: delegatemany is an advertised first-party model tool in the normal loop, and the allocator and child caps run when this optional standard mode is chosen with a configured token budget. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the parent model chooses to fan out tasks but does not decide the concrete equal-share allocation or dynamically renegotiate exhausted quotas; its choice of delegates does not transfer ownership of the coordination response. - Evidence: src/tools/delegatemany.ts, src/budget.ts, src/subagents.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: the budget mechanism is inactive when no parent token budget is configured; children are read-only and thus do not create a qualifying shared-file write conflict. No generalized coordination beyond bounded token contention is claimed.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no distinct whole-current resource/commitment management over a population of independently accountable operational units is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established for an S3-specific whole-current function. - Whole-system current view: the current run tracks tool/child usage and process-scoped background command status, not an enduring portfolio of S1 commitments under a separate manager. - Current-control decision scope: the main Agent delegates task fragments, handles tool feedback and can stop background shell processes; these are local operational choices, not a whole-system current resource bargain or intervention authority. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no material S3-specific right established. - Decision owner: no S3 owner established; configured caps belong to the operator/developer and routine task choices to S1. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: budget counters, todo state, concurrency ceilings, background process map/status, timeouts, token limits and permission gates. - Closure path: no whole-current performance/resource regulation returned across an enduring S1 portfolio; deterministic limits simply bound the selected run and its child operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-selected delegation and fixed resource enforcement do not constitute autonomous S3 current control; similarly a human set ceiling is not a complete parent S3 mode. - Evidence: src/loop.ts, background tasks, src/budget.ts. - Basis: structural bounded absence review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the TUI exposes process-local task status and operator stopping controls; they are not credited as a whole-current governance regime over autonomous S1 commitments.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge a coding result through a first-party adversarial code-review specialist with direct source evidence and returned findings. - Disturbance / variety regulated: defects and risky assumptions missed by the main coding actor&#39;s own reasoning or self-report. - Claim being audited: the correctness/security and failure behavior of a proposed repository change. - Ordinary reporting path: the main coding Agent&#39;s own tool observations, implementation summary, and regular command/test feedback. - Complementary access path: the built-in review subagent receives a fresh transcript, specialist adversarial prompt and read/search tools to inspect actual workspace files, then reports grounded file:line issues. - Independence boundary: a distinct read-only child model loop cannot edit the reviewed repository, sees no parent transcript, and applies a separate risk-focused prompt; it is product-internal complementary independence, not an external third-party review. - Audit judgment / decisive feedback right: select concrete correctness/security findings and their severity from direct code evidence rather than accepting the parent Agent&#39;s report. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the reviewer model&#39;s source-grounded risk judgment and corrective feedback returned to the parent. - Decision owner: the built-in review subagent model in the opted-in delegated-review mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: agent definitions, read-only plan-mode, no-network/delegation child caps, path jail, timeout, summary return, audit chain and per-child usage accounting. - How findings enter subsequent control: the delegate tool returns the specialist&#39;s findings as a parent tool result in the main model transcript, making them actionable in its next coding steps. - Who acts on findings: the main coding Agent decides whether and how to implement corrections from the returned review; optional human permission gates still govern protected edits. - Closure path: primary code artifact → separate read-only reviewer inspects files → independent risk report with file:line findings → main Agent receives report and can correct/test subsequent work. - Boundary reachability: review is one of the built-in shipped agents in src/subagents.ts and is reachable via advertised delegate/delegatemany model tools, with runtime read-only enforcement. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the review model leaves the deterministic audit log and tool filters, but not the independent semantic risk assessment; the model owns that audit judgment in the supported optional mode. - Evidence: src/subagents.ts, src/tools/delegate.ts, src/tools/child-run.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: review is opt-in rather than a mandatory gate, findings are plain returned text rather than a dedicated persistent review database, and the main model may disregard them; no independently demonstrated effectiveness is claimed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no supported external-and-prospective capability adaptation loop is established within the installed coding runtime. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established for an S4-specific future environmental adaptation function. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no established prospective adaptation selection and return. - Decision owner: not established in the assessed runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model/provider routing and failover, session memory, remembered approvals, outcome metrics, operator verdicts, benchmark/immunity analysis and roadmap papers. - Closure path: no product-run environmental sensing → future options → changed current capability/control cycle was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model changes during execution address current provider failures; developer benchmark and immunity suites are adjacent evaluation, not a live strategic S4 owner. - Evidence: src/loop.ts, src/remember.ts, src/verdict.ts. - Basis: structural bounded absence review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: outcomes and retrospective verdicts could inform a future learning system but do not by themselves constitute a product-wired prospective adaptation decision.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party ultimate identity/constitutional policy authority and returned decision path are established at the installed harness recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool permissions, risky commands, private-route selection and remembered/promoted denies are operational constraints, not an identity-level controversy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no identity/ultimate-policy decision right established. - Decision owner: developer and operator choose configuration/permission rules outside a qualifying S5 product loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: non-overridable denylist, policy file, manual approvals, session grants, remembered shapes, promoted denies, audit signatures and local trust commands. - Closure path: external permissions/configuration → deterministic policy check → tool allow/ask/deny is present; identity-level matter → legitimate authority decision → authoritative return governing the organization is not. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: neither fixed policy enforcement nor an operator&#39;s approval of an ordinary file/bash command establishes ultimate identity/policy governance by a first-party actor. - Evidence: src/policy.ts, src/policy-store.ts, src/audit.ts. - Basis: structural bounded absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a human can approve persistent tool-denial promotion, but that is task/tool risk policy without a demonstrable system identity/ultimate-policy adjudication return.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/61d2fe17cc3d8b1a12c1a754a12deda1e6242348/assessments/co/codewhip.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 61d2fe17cc3d8b1a12c1a754a12deda1e6242348\">61d2fe1 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-24T16:24:36+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/sppideey/ucode-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ucode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-24\">2026-10-24</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/sppideey/ucode-agent/tree/be41840d1219ea45490ac8c61ba84fa48d3119a6\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision be41840d1219ea45490ac8c61ba84fa48d3119a6\">be41840 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: adapt coding and app-construction decisions to actual source files, tests, tool outputs and user feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing code structure, runtime errors, missing dependencies, edit conflicts, provider interruptions, generated-app behavior and requested features. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select tool calls, concrete file modifications, task decomposition and follow-on fixes based on returned current evidence. - Decision owner: primary ucode model-backed agent; each optional worker separately chooses its assigned coding actions in its own model/tool loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: source tools, project memory/session, typecheck/tests, app browser, fallback policy, permissions/confirmations, tool time bounds, plan UI and worker summaries. - Closure path: coding objective + source context → main or worker model selects actions → first-party tool execution → result/type/test/error feedback → model revises or ends. - Boundary reachability: src/core/loop.js and advertised first-party tool schemas reach real file/shell actions in the normal CLI and supported worker mode, rather than relying solely on prompt assertions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic tools can execute a requested command but cannot independently choose and revise open-ended code against varied task evidence; that right belongs to model turns. - Evidence: core/loop.js, tools/index.js, core/provider.js. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: script tests establish wiring but do not measure live provider reliability or code quality.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate concurrent worker interference over one mutable project filesystem and local command/process resources. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple coding S1 workers may simultaneously rewrite files or start conflicting install/server commands in the same workspace, causing interleaved writes, dependency-state conflicts or shared-port races. - Distinct S1 units: up to three independent worker model/tool loops, each with a separate task, context, model response sequence and concrete shared-workspace actions. - Inter-S1 disturbance: a worker&#39;s file or shell effects can invalidate another&#39;s expected state, shared command sequence or running process resource. - Attenuating coordination relation: the common Agent.fileLock Promise chain serializes FILEWRITES and runcommand(s) for all workers while allowing independent read/investigation work to proceed. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: each child receives its tool result after the lock-held operation and revises subsequent model steps; parent receives touched-file lists, child status and warnings if a worker wrote nothing. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: a shared exclusive effect domain across concurrently acting independent worker models is actively attenuated, not merely a sequential one-model tool batch or message queue. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide the specific inter-worker effect-order policy and release conditions; the shipped deterministic lock implements that fixed right. - Decision owner: developers&#39; fixed source-code lock policy; no autonomous S2 actor observes conflicts and renegotiates write scopes or resource priorities. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: MAXWORKERS=3, worker-only tool restriction, Promise.all child execution, exclusive fileLock for writes/shell, worker result receipts and post-worker touched-file reconciliation. - Closure path: simultaneous worker effects → shared lock queues mutation/shell operations → actual serial outcome/error returns per child → worker actions adapt; aggregate outcomes return to lead for integration. - Boundary reachability: delegate is an advertised main tool; worker loops and fileLock are connected through shipped normal coding execution, not a test harness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: parent model selects decomposition, but fixed shared-effect exclusion is imposed deterministically; model choice of worker tasks does not make allocation/lock arbitration autonomous. - Evidence: fileLock/delegate/runWorker in core/loop.js. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: lock serializes file writes and certain commands, not all possible side effects; prompt-only file ownership can fail, and actual protection against conflicting high-level edits is not empirically measured.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no distinct whole-current resource/commitment authority over a durable population of independently accountable operational projects is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: delegation, worker summaries and user-visible plans relate to the currently assigned coding task, not a whole-current portfolio of independent operating commitments. - Whole-system current view: the main model constructs a task split, gets worker summaries after Promise.all, and sees touched files; the UI shows task status, but no persistent whole-current performance/resource model across separate viable S1 jobs. - Current-control decision scope: the main model chooses file/task decomposition and integrates completed output; resource reallocation during independent worker execution is limited to fixed concurrency/lock and operator cancellation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no distinct S3 resource bargain or whole-current commitment-management right established apart from S1 task composition. - Decision owner: primary operational coding model for current task; operator may stop/steer; no separate S3 owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: updateplan, worker task name/result summaries, touched-file receipts, MAXWORKERS, shared lock and user UI. - Closure path: model task split → child work → parent integration exists; independent whole-current operation performance → central resource/commitment decisions → returned corrective operating controls is not established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the lead model is autonomous at task planning, but this alone does not establish an independent viable-system S3 over a standing operational population. - Evidence: delegate/updatePlan and runWorker, README.md. - Basis: structural bounded absence review. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: a different recursion boundary treating a single transient build as an entire multi-worker operating organization might expose S3-like lead behavior; the published claim does not inflate current task delegation into independent whole-current control.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge a generated application&#39;s visible usability/design claims through first-party browser evidence and a separately prompted visual model reviewer. - Disturbance / variety regulated: visual layout defects, weak hierarchy, inaccessible controls or broken user flows that ordinary code writing and test output can miss. - Claim being audited: the working coding Agent&#39;s app should look and behave acceptably on desktop and phone screens. - Ordinary reporting path: main Agent reads source, build/type/test outputs and automatic browser probes; it may describe its own app. - Complementary access path: the /look tool opens the running local app in Playwright browser, captures real phone/desktop screenshots and diagnostics, then a separate vision-capable model judges visual problems from screenshots under an independent designer prompt. - Independence boundary: the visual reviewer sees image evidence and its own review prompt, not merely the author&#39;s text; it cannot edit the source and is invoked in a separate provider model call. This is product-internal complementary independence, not an external human audit. - Audit judgment / decisive feedback right: identify and prioritize specific visual/design issues and fixes from observed screenshots, not just parse routine test exit status. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the separately instantiated vision review model owns the semantic visual critique; deterministic browser probes provide additional direct observations. - Decision owner: the built-in visual review model on the opt-in /look path, constrained by product prompt and model time budget. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Playwright local browser, width-specific captures, console/layout/a11y probes, 60-second independent review budget, per-URL retry limiter, output formatting. - How findings enter subsequent control: cmdLook writes the report for the user and pushes it as a user-message evidence entry to the primary Agent history; the coding model can respond and repair on following turns. - Who acts on findings: main coding Agent under user task direction; visual reviewer itself cannot directly mutate the app. - Closure path: produced app → browser screenshot and usage/console probes → separate vision-model critique → cmdLook injects findings into main conversation → subsequent implementation/repair can use that complementary evidence. - Boundary reachability: /look is a shipped first-party user command invoking lookAtApp with default review:true. Automatic handOver/lookOnceThisTurn paths explicitly request review:false, so the credited S3 mode is optional and user-triggered. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the semantic design judgment belongs to a distinct vision model; fixed DOM checks alone are routine verification, and the author model does not produce the reviewer prompt answer. - Evidence: browser.js, cmdLook and lookOnceThisTurn in loop.js. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: opt-in only, depends on local Chrome/Edge, Playwright and provider availability; failed review is skipped, reviewer effectiveness is not measured and the main model is not forced to adopt recommendations.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no product-owned horizon-sensing and prospective organizational capability choice/adoption loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: provider stalls, task context size, past session records and developer performance-tracing evidence are handled for immediate reliability or by maintainers outside the shipped runtime. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party strategy for changing future capabilities using assessed environment options and adoption closure. - Decision owner: developers configure model/tool features and standing skills; a static runtime fallback handles current failed providers. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: UCODETRACE, model fallbacks, stuck watch, project memory, auto-update and developer measured before/after accounts. - Closure path: current task/provider feedback → retry/fallback and context/tool control exists; horizon sensing → evaluated future option → adopted capability/policy feedback inside the product is absent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model failover and retrospective maintainer performance improvements do not turn the coding Agent into an organizational strategy owner. - Evidence: provider.js, context.js, README.md. - Basis: structural bounded absence review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: developer experiments and trace-driven engineering should not be imported as autonomous S4 ownership of the frozen installed harness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party legitimate ultimate-identity and purpose policy adjudication with authoritative organizational return. - Disturbance / variety regulated: file risks, model modes, secret scanning before deployment and user action confirmations constrain specific coding operations, not ultimate viable-system identity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no constitutional organizational decision beyond task-specific tool approvals and supplied instructions. - Decision owner: user/developer determine configuration, deploy initiation and skill policy; main model executes bounded tasks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: scoped project paths, tool offering/plan-mode refusals, protected deploy command, secret checks and human confirmation path. - Closure path: external task permission → allow/deny and ordinary workflow updates exists; purpose/identity controversy → authoritative S5 adjudication → organization-wide decision return is absent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deployment approval and secret protection are important safety operations, but do not constitute a legitimate ultimate-policy governing authority. - Evidence: core/scope.js, tools/deploy.js, core/loop.js. - Basis: structural bounded absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: ultimate human governance of an actual development team is outside the assessed ucode product boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/14c89bdaea97c7e8d22359f804390ba4b10e48f1/assessments/uc/ucode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 14c89bdaea97c7e8d22359f804390ba4b10e48f1\">14c89bd &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-10T20:02:22+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Maurdekye/orgtree\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Orgtree</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-10\">2026-10-10</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Maurdekye/orgtree/tree/0008ccb94edfb986396c49ced3e2d2c885e1fb16\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 0008ccb94edfb986396c49ced3e2d2c885e1fb16\">0008ccb &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute bounded coding/investigation tasks through separately instantiated provider-backed autonomous working agents, supported by Orgtree persistent organizations and tooling. - Disturbance / variety regulated: evolving user tasks, codebase complexity, failing tests, external model/tool feedback and revised instructions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: each hired model-driven agent chooses steps and code/tool activity within its charter, provider and granted scope. - Decision owner: selected Claude/Codex/OpenRouter-model agent actor hosted by Orgtree; private provider cognition is an external implementation, not Orgtree-authored organizational logic. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persistent agent nodes, charters, provider adapters and session runners, tool/folder grants, CLI/MCP tools and queued conversation. - Closure path: user/coordinator supplies job and charter → model-driven agent starts under configured provider → model makes work/tool decisions against repo/task feedback → changes/response reach retained transcript and docket → next local agent step reflects updated context. - Boundary reachability: the Windows packaged engine + desktop explicitly creates agents, starts their model turns and offers first-party OpenRouter/provider adapters; not a development-only agent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the model-driven actor Orgtree has no comparable discretionary task-solving capability; retaining a chart and provider launch without inference only transports work. - Evidence: README.md; supervisor.py; openrouterharness.py; openrouter.py; providers.py; mcptool.py - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for assembled installed provider mode. - Caveats: OpenRouter model inference and external coding-agent internal tool execution are third-party; Orgtree&#39;s own persistent control wiring is the credited harness boundary.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate conflicting resource ownership by separate operating agents attempting concurrent work or integration. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two active workers may try to land incompatible changes or mutate the same shared resource/candidate/base under separate responsibilities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: grant one held reservation and refuse a competing claimant while live owner/heartbeat exists, with scoped recovery only after staleness proof. - Decision owner: deterministic first-party resource reservation handler; workers/coordinator select target resources but the conflict gate is constructor-enforced, not autonomously resolved. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persistent reservation rows under document lock, resource key, candidate/base pairing, owner identity, lease/heartbeat, error projection of holder, renewal/release/land. - Closure path: agent A acquires scoped shared resource → agent B requests same resource with incompatible ownership → first-party ReservationError names still-live holder and refuses B → B must defer/reconcile work, preventing duplicate land → release/recovery makes resource available for subsequent work. - Boundary reachability: reservation module is in shipped Python engine&#39;s org document/API control path and is described as runtime durable scoped resource reservations, with standard agent-facing coordination tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the decisive arbitration is deterministic; there is no independent agent choosing winners. This is S2=C for a specific inter-worker collision, not generic organization-chart messaging. - Distinct S1 units: two independently hired provider-backed coding-agent nodes working toward commits against one shared repository/integration resource. - Inter-S1 disturbance: simultaneous integrations or mutable resource claims create conflicting ownership and duplicate/stale actions. - Attenuating coordination relation: reservations.execute(action=acquire) checks active holder, normalized resource and scope, denies different live claimant unless holder actually ceased; lease alone never steals live claim. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: refusal returns holder/resource and reason to calling agent, so it defers or selects other work; only a valid held actor can perform related mutation/landing. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: a concrete inter-S1 exclusive-resource conflict is damped at admission rather than relying on ordinary mail, hierarchical titles, queue ordering, or shared storage. - Evidence: reservations.py; api.py; ledger.py; mcptool.py; README.md - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium-high for scoped reservation mode. - Caveats: reservation metadata does not itself sandbox files, and overlap query is advisory for declared paths; credit is restricted to exclusive resource ownership arbitration.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: whole-current organization control by a designated persistent coordinator agent managing job assignment, staffing, review gates and blocked work across subordinate agent units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: duplicate staffing, missing/blocked assignments, forgotten post-compaction decisions and unattended review/implementation tasks across active workers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the configured coordinator model selects whether to create/assign/reassign docket tasks, hire staff with work, inspect subordinate evidence, or escalate disputed facts to user. - Decision owner: an explicitly hired model-backed coordinator in the supported first-party preset/agent-management mode; neither static org hierarchy nor credit checks autonomously owns these choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: bundled coordinator charter, first-party orgtreestaff, orgtreework, hire/rehire, task ownership notifications, direct chat/mail, active org/docket state and transcript view. - Closure path: coordinator sees user request and persistent docket/agent/tree state → interprets workload/ownership/report evidence → decides which unit should perform/review work → first-party staffing/assignment API writes owner and delivers wake instruction → worker&#39;s next agent turn executes and returns progress/evidence. - Boundary reachability: README explicitly supports a human hiring a coordinator and bundled charter presets; live agent-facing orgtreestaff tool performs atomic seat+work assignment, and associated hire/work operations are in the packaged engine. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the coordinator model exercises discretion over present organizational allocations; removing it leaves only human input and deterministic staffing APIs, not equivalent automated current control. - Whole-system current view: coordinator can inspect the parent org tree and its own subordinates, shared docket, messages, transcripts and status/evidence; the charter directs it to check for already running work before staffing. - Current-control decision scope: decide who owns each new feature/investigation, when a review is required, who responds to a blocked specialist, and when to retain/retire agent seats. - Evidence: README.md; coordinator.md; mcptool.py; api.py; ledger.py; supervisor.py - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high in supported coordinator operating mode. - Caveats: the coordinator is optional and must be hired/chartered; a passive org chart and basic hierarchy alone do not yield S3=A, and no credit is given to a private provider team manager.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: ? - Function: independent challenge of ordinary operational/implementation reports with direct evidence and corrective feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: implementation claims that misstate source changes, tests or production behavior. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the review-workflow charter proposes distinct redteam reviewer roles and coordinator approval, but a guaranteed separately instantiated independent audit-and-return closure is not shown for all supported organizations. - Decision owner: undetermined for first-party enacted S3; optional model reviewer would own judgment only if explicitly hired, given direct evidence, and returned findings alter later work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: review charter preset, code/test evidence, docket review items, transcript/file inspection and work-evidence git provenance. - Closure path: a reviewer may inspect implementation and report findings; actual invocation, independence of reviewer from implementer and mandatory correction/acceptance loop remain mode-dependent and were not reconstructed to publication threshold. - Boundary reachability: review charter and tooling ship, but user-selected workflow alone does not prove operational closure in current assessment boundary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a role label or proposed review checklist cannot substitute for an evidenced independent, different decision owner and a corrective return. - Claim being audited: agent implementing a feature has delivered correct code. - Ordinary reporting path: implementer conversation, docket progress and claimed completion. - Complementary access path: reviewer direct source/commit/test inspection and runtime work-evidence tools are possible. - Independence boundary: role separation is configurable rather than guaranteed; no canonical independent audit workflow bound in all modes. - Who acts on findings: coordinator/user may require changes, but obligatory first-party return from independent findings is not conclusively established. - Evidence: review-workflow.md; coordinator.md; workitems.py; workevidence.py; mcptool.py - Basis: explicit + unresolved. - Confidence: medium in uncertain closure. - Caveats: do not turn the charter&#39;s redteam guidance into positive S3 on documentation alone; retain ?.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: ? - Function: prospective monitoring of environmental possibilities with chosen adaptation options that modify future organization capabilities. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changes in provider models, incoming work and external demands not yet handled by current staffing. - Decisive decision or feedback right: operator can change providers/accounts/charters; reactive account fallback and persisted history do not show independently owned prospective environment scanning and adaptation choices. - Decision owner: unresolved. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persistent agent history, provider availability, account fallback, model selection and handoff. - Closure path: switch to another account may allow an interrupted turn to finish, but no evidential prospective exploration → future capability-change decision → return into current operations is established. - Boundary reachability: provider/continuity components ship; positive S4 not inferred from them. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: resilience/fallback is a reactive resource workaround rather than outside-and-then strategic intelligence. - External distinction: external model/provider availability changes are observed reactively. - Future / prospective distinction: no separately evidenced future-option research, beyond current-task orchestration. - Adaptation option generated: unverified. - Path back into current capability / S3: no S4-specific returned adaptation established. - Evidence: providers.py; accounts.py; handoff.py; README.md - Basis: structural + unresolved. - Confidence: medium in insufficient evidence. - Caveats: future configured roles might act prospectively, but no such supported first-party loop was proven at this frozen ref.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: ? - Function: decide ultimate purpose and identity of the whole organization when constitutive conflicts arise. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changes to legitimate goals, final authority and organizational identity rather than ordinary permission/staffing. - Decisive decision or feedback right: human operator sets charters and capacity/access constraints, but no distinct ultimate-policy issue→authority→returned operation was reconstructed. - Decision owner: human owner configures charter/permissions and deployment boundaries; distinct S5 ownership unresolved. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persistent agent identities, charter text, hierarchy, folder/tool grants, capacity and delegation budget, audience request path. - Closure path: charters and permissions affect later agent execution, but this is normal configuration rather than evidence of an enacted identity-level decision loop. - Boundary reachability: charter/edit controls are standard shipped mode; no positive S5 based on them. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an autonomous coordinator following a founder&#39;s charter is not authorized to define or change the organization&#39;s ultimate identity by being called &#39;coordinator&#39;. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: not established as a resolved runtime issue. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: no positive S5 parent or autonomous mode demonstrated. - Return-to-operation path: config reaches agent prompts and enforcement but does not prove whole-purpose adjudication. - Evidence: README.md; schema.py; mcptool.py; api.py; coordinator.md - Basis: structural + unresolved. - Confidence: medium in insufficient evidence. - Caveats: persisted roles and delegated grants are not organizational ultimate identity decisions.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/5f4e494b65b62449b1c3199d40892c08bb78c78b/assessments/orgtree.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 5f4e494b65b62449b1c3199d40892c08bb78c78b\">5f4e494 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-28T18:43:06+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/showlab/Show-Harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Show-Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-28\">2026-10-28</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/showlab/Show-Harness/tree/6ad77dbc6180e973dff8148ffcca6c085580cea2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 6ad77dbc6180e973dff8148ffcca6c085580cea2\">6ad77db &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: transform visual manipulation instructions into executed robot actions and completed subgoals. - Disturbance / variety regulated: scene changes, object position, missed grasps, malformed action responses and incomplete manipulation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose subsequent atomic action and judge stage completion from current observations. - Decision owner: the first-party controller/planner VLM roles in the launched rollout. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: camera sessions, token parsing, atomic interpreters, physical safety floors, step limits and recovery state. - Closure path: VLM token → interpreter action → fresh scene/proprioception and recovery context → next VLM action or completion/replan. - Boundary reachability: core/launch.py directly wires the shipped roles, prompts and real/dual runners; users provide model/hardware configuration, not a custom decision organization. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the VLM leaves token transport and fixed recovery but removes discretionary visual action choice. - Evidence: launch, real runner, VLM roles, recovery. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for shipped decision wiring. - Caveats: no physical robot or model-provider run; no effectiveness or safety certification is inferred.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: regulate interference between two concurrently acting manipulation tracks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: both grippers entering a small destination, unsafe simultaneous contact with one object, and premature release during handover. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select which arm waits and when the visible condition permits it to resume. - Decision owner: the dual planner/controller VLM, applying the shipped coordination instructions to live joint observations. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-arm stage state, WAIT descriptions, STILL tokens and concurrent interpreter execution. - Closure path: shared scene and both arm states → VLM WAIT/STILL/progress judgment → one arm holds while the other acts → changed visible handover/destination state → resumed action. - Boundary reachability: the dual launch/runner uses DualSubgoalPlanner and DualControllerAgent with shipped dual prompts, without developer-authored coordination logic. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: waiting completion is visually interpreted by the VLM rather than a runner-side hard gate; runtime executes the chosen response. - Distinct S1 units: left and right arm manipulation tracks, each with its own subgoals, recent moves, recovery, completion and actuator/environment interaction. - Inter-S1 disturbance: structurally evidenced shared-small-destination contention and giver/receiver handover interference. - Attenuating coordination relation: planner inserts conditional WAIT stages; the joint controller selects STILL and orders delivery/contact according to current images. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: satisfying the visible wait condition changes the next arm token and advances that arm&#39;s track. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: explicit collision/handover conditions determine mutual inhibition and resumption, beyond merely splitting a task into two lists. - Evidence: dual runner, dual controller, dual planning prompt, dual action prompt. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: this mapping is specific to supported dual-arm mode; it does not establish a general multi-agent coordination service or measured collision avoidance reliability.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no distinct whole-system resource/commitment regulation path established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: step exhaustion, incomplete task and grasp failure are handled as local rollout execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: task planning/replanning belongs to operational roles; step/replan caps and safety constraints are selected by configuration/operator. - Decision owner: VLM for operational choices; operator/configuration for fixed constraints, not a shipped S3 owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-stage caps, maxreplans, interpreter limits and DAgger preemption. - Closure path: bounded operational retry/override; no function-specific whole-system bargaining/intervention loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: neither autonomous S3 discretion nor a closed parent S3 mode follows from task-level action intervention. - Evidence: dual final-check/replan and caps, launch constraints, DAgger. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: joint scene visibility is not by itself S3 current-control authority.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no complementary independent operational audit path established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: routine completion and grounding errors are checked within production. - Decisive decision or feedback right: final scene verification and affordance grounding correction. - Decision owner: production VLM/checking roles and deterministic sensor recovery; no independent audit owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: saved episode images, measured gripper widths and final-task verification. - Closure path: ordinary retry/replan/re-grounding, without complementary challenge of the operational reporting arrangement. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an agent making a mandatory production check does not alone establish S3. - Evidence: final verification, affordance agent, episode logger. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: raw images can support a later external audit, which is not supplied by this rollout boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no external-and-prospective capability adaptation loop established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: live scene changes and task-specific calibration remain present operational variety. - Decisive decision or feedback right: adapt the current action/plan to the observed task. - Decision owner: operational VLM; offline model/data selection remains developer work outside this runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: recent-move memory, video-reference briefs, action-effect tables and replanning. - Closure path: feedback changes this rollout&#39;s actions, not a future-environment capability option returned into current regulation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: learning a task&#39;s action effects or following a video is insufficient for the specific S4 function. - Evidence: video reference, action ablation, deepplan. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: offline training results do not supply deployed adaptation ownership.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy decision-return loop established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: configured safety and action bounds constrain manipulation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: operator chooses task, backend, rig limits and intervention actions. - Decision owner: operator/developer configuration; not an evidenced function-specific S5 arrangement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: prompts, allowed-token schemas, safety floors, configuration layering and interruption. - Closure path: direct constraint execution; no identity-level issue referral and authoritative return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: action discretion is bounded by policy but does not own ultimate policy. - Evidence: configuration, launch, DAgger. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: operator authority exists without qualifying runtime S5 closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/0bc351dbb2d4f2b27b4e0685df0ae49616643be6/assessments/sh/show-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 0bc351dbb2d4f2b27b4e0685df0ae49616643be6\">0bc351d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-24T17:03:31+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Vinay-veeragani/Multi-Agent-Orchestration\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Multi-Agent Orchestration Engine</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-24\">2026-09-24</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Vinay-veeragani/Multi-Agent-Orchestration/tree/e6c34462af045d7e53d383103346362351c96353\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e6c34462af045d7e53d383103346362351c96353\">e6c3446 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform assigned operational task work through autonomous agent reason/act loops that can choose tool use and produce task outputs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task uncertainty, model/tool feedback, external data returned by allowed tools and local decisions required to satisfy each delegated instruction. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose task-local reasoning steps, permitted tool calls and when to return the assigned result. - Decision owner: the autonomous model actor inside each first-party AgentRuntime invocation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentDefinition, model routing, tool schemas, policy authoriser, budget checks, iteration limits, tool execution and output validation. - Closure path: supervisor/workflow assigns a node instruction → AgentRuntime builds the agent context → autonomous model reasons and requests permitted tools → tool results feed back into the same loop → the agent returns a validated AgentOutput into execution state. - Boundary reachability: the shared AgentRuntime is the standard execution path for registered agents, including dynamically delegated nodes; it is not a benchmark-only or development-only actor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic runtime code limits and transports the loop, but substantive task-local reasoning/tool choices are made by the model actor. - Evidence: src/orchestration/agents/runtime.py; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: provider/model internals are environmental; ownership is credited only to the autonomous role reached through the first-party runtime.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party run-level S2 coordination function is established at the reviewed boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the runtime contains parallel execution, joins, locks, semaphores and dependency scheduling, but the inspected standard paths do not establish a specific inter-S1 interference/conflict/oscillation together with a function-specific coordination decision/feedback path that changes subsequent autonomous agent behavior. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S2. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: workflow edges and join policies sequence/aggregate work; maxconcurrentnodes limits parallel execution; Redis locks/semaphores protect process-level mutual exclusion and capacity; supervisor delegation controls current work allocation. These are not promoted to S2 merely because they coordinate execution machinery. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewed runtime has generic scheduling, synchronization and current-control mechanisms, but no evidenced S2-specific autonomous coordination discretion over a reconstructed inter-S1 disturbance. - Evidence: docs/workflow-engine.md; src/orchestration/coordination/redis.py; docs/dynamic-orchestration.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a downstream workflow can use the supplied synchronization primitives to construct stronger coordination behavior; general framework expressiveness is not sufficient for S2=C.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the current execution as a whole by observing live run state and deciding which agents/work should run next, when failed work should retry or be replanned, when parallel work should be introduced, and when the run should finalize or fail. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete or failed work, changing execution progress, unsuitable delegation, exhausted/invalid routes, current resource/budget constraints, blocked work and uncertainty about whether more operational work is required. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose a RoutingDecision over delegate, paralleldelegate, retry, replan, requesthumanapproval, responddirectly, finalize or fail against the current ExecutionState. - Decision owner: the autonomous supervisor model actor in the standard dynamic-orchestration mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: typed routing schema, semantic validation, agent registry, deterministic fallback, ExecutionOrchestrator, workflow compilation/execution, budget meter, policy engine, checkpoint/event persistence and cancel token. - Closure path: current execution/node state is supplied to Supervisor.decide() → autonomous supervisor selects a current-control action → first-party schema/semantic validators accept or degrade it → ExecutionOrchestrator compiles/applies the valid action → agents/nodes execute under that decision → resulting node state/output returns to the next supervisor turn. - Boundary reachability: ExecutionOrchestrator explicitly calls the first-party Supervisor each turn in the supported dynamic execution path; this is the ordinary runtime design rather than an evaluator-only layer. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the supervisor model owns the discretionary current-control choice when it produces a valid decision. Deterministic validators, schedulers, budgets and policy gates enforce feasibility/constraints without inheriting the decision right. The heuristic fallback is a degraded deterministic mode rather than the basis for A. - Evidence: src/orchestration/supervisor/supervisor.py; docs/supervisor-and-routing.md; docs/dynamic-orchestration.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: static user-authored workflows and deterministic heuristic fallback are separate supported control paths; the A finding is specifically grounded in the standard autonomous dynamic-supervisor mode. Action-scoped human approval does not by itself establish an S3 parent mode. - Whole-system current view: the supervisor prompt is built from current ExecutionState, registered agent summaries, tool surface and optional budget/workflow state; it can validate retries/replans against actual node status and current graph state. - Current-control decision scope: delegation/allocation, parallel fan-out, retry, graph replan, approval escalation, direct response, finalization and failure of the current execution.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no materially distinct complementary audit path over operational S1 reality is established in the reviewed standard distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: routing/schema/policy validators can reject invalid decisions or calls, but they inspect conformance of the same control/tool path rather than independently challenge operational claims/results against alternative evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S3. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Pydantic/schema validation, Supervisor.validatedecision(), policy checks, output validation, checkpoint consistency and benchmark/evaluation code provide correctness/enforcement surfaces but do not become S3 by label or verification vocabulary. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewed validators are in-line deterministic gates around ordinary operation/current control; no separate autonomous or constructor audit actor with complementary access and corrective feedback into the same run was found. - Evidence: src/orchestration/supervisor/supervisor.py; docs/supervisor-and-routing.md; src/orchestration/agents/runtime.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: repository evaluation/benchmark infrastructure is adjacent first-party evidence and was excluded from runtime ownership under Methodology 0.3.6 boundary rules.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no external-and-prospective organizational adaptation loop is established in the reviewed standard distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: retry/replan/model routing respond to the current task/run, and checkpoints preserve current execution state, but none establishes a separate outside/future sensing → adaptation option → returned capability change loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S4. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: supervisor replan, node retry policies, model routing, durable checkpoints/resume, configuration and benchmark/evaluation infrastructure support current execution/recovery without establishing prospective adaptation. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current-run replanning is S3/current-control behavior; no first-party actor is evidenced as changing persistent organizational capability from external/future distinctions. - Evidence: docs/dynamic-orchestration.md; docs/checkpointing-and-resume.md; docs/evaluation-benchmark.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: retries, replanning and benchmark-driven development may improve the project in practice; they are not automatically run-level S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy governance loop is established at the assessed execution boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: configured budgets, tool permissions, risk rules and approval gates constrain operation, but they are pre-existing operational policy/enforcement rather than an authoritative decision over organizational identity or ultimate policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S5. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: deny-by-default tool policy, hard budgets, risk-level approval requirements, operator-supplied configuration and action-scoped human approvals. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the runtime enforces configured rules and can pause for action-specific approval, but no first-party standard mode was found in which an autonomous or parent authority adjudicates an identity/ultimate-policy question and returns a newly decided policy into subsequent organization-wide operation. - Evidence: docs/budget-and-policies.md; docs/human-in-the-loop.md; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a human may approve or modify one risky tool action; Methodology 0.3.6 explicitly does not promote generic operational approval to S5 parent governance.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/13ed3855b7ea0d890c83aadea7732422ca8db7f2/assessments/multi-agent-orchestration.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 13ed3855b7ea0d890c83aadea7732422ca8db7f2\">13ed385 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-11T22:54:20+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Omerfaruk-aydn/Atlas-Agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Atlas Agent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-11\">2026-10-11</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Omerfaruk-aydn/Atlas-Agent/tree/3d0d63e20d9d2221be9a6e6ab4fc0350df5237f5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3d0d63e20d9d2221be9a6e6ab4fc0350df5237f5\">3d0d63e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-directed coding operations in the first-party Go session runtime; includes shell/filesystem/source tools, provider/tool feedback and independently model-driven bounded subagent instances. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing user instructions, source/code errors, build/environment changes, provider responses and tool results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Main SessionAgent model selects work and tools after observing files, commands and previous responses; bounded subagent model chooses its own scoped actions. - Decision owner: First-party sessionAgent model loop, or generated model-driven child on delegated work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Coordinator model/provider builder, registered toolset, session persistence, cancel and message queue, prompt hooks, budgets and permission gates. - Closure path: User goal → model Stream/tool selection → tool execution/result → next model step or reply, with persistent messages and bounded recovery. - Boundary reachability: Shipped Atlas executable uses Coordinator.Run with buildAgent; it constructs SessionAgent and first-party worktree tools. The user need not bring an external coding-agent CLI as the actor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Without first-party model-selected actions the fixed tool registry and turn controls do not decide which code fix satisfies the user. - Evidence: internal/agent/coordinator.go, internal/agent/coordinator.go, internal/agent/agent.go, internal/agent/agent.go - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Prevent provider-wide rate-limit interference among parallel model-driven child S1 sessions launched by one task organization. - Distinct S1 units: Multiple independently executing SessionAgent children with individually selected named task prompts created by the agent/delegate/orchestrate/debate tool paths. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Simultaneous provider calls from a large fan-out cause shared service throttling (429 bursts), degrading each child&#39;s progress; source explicitly calls out this concrete failure mode. - Attenuating coordination relation: Configured MaxConcurrentSubAgents creates a channel-semaphore admission gate shared across child runs within the respective delegation tool; waiting children pause instead of starting simultaneous provider use. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: A child acquires a slot or cancels while queued; after a previous child releases its slot, admitted child performs its model/tool loop; results are returned to the caller to inform subsequent task decisions. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: This is not merely selecting an agent or forwarding messages: concurrent independently model-driven S1 work would hit a concrete shared-provider overload disturbance; the constructor dynamically gates and releases admission before subsequent child execution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Inter-child request concurrency and provider 429 overload within a configured rate-limited environment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Admit/wait/cancel child execution based on a concurrency slot, then release the slot after completion; the upper model selects subtask goals but does not adjudicate slot policy. - Decision owner: Deterministic first-party concurrencyLimiter constructor (C) rather than an autonomous S2 decision model. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Go channel semaphore, tool-level limiter, cancellation token, WaitGroup/join and result multiplexing. - Closure path: Concurrent child requests → slot classification/admission or wait → provider calls serialized to capacity → release on finish → queued child proceeds, returns result to parent. - Boundary reachability: agent, orchestrate, delegate, and debate tool builders register limiter when maxconcurrentsubagents is set above zero; this is packaged Atlas code, not an external team orchestrator. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agent decides decomposition, but deterministic runtime alone enforces the response to rate-limit contention. - Evidence: internal/agent/subagentlimit.go, internal/agent/orchestratetool.go, internal/agent/orchestratetool.go, internal/agent/delegatetool.go, internal/agent/agenttool.go, internal/config/config.go - Basis: explicit / structural - Confidence: high for constructor, medium for measured effect - Caveats: An unconfigured (zero) cap is unlimited; positive C is explicitly bounded to deployments setting a finite cap. It does not prevent semantic write conflicts between children.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No separately owned organization-wide discretionary supervisor responsible for continuously reallocating priorities/resources across operational S1 teams. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Session cost, tool permissions, user goals, concurrent prompts, retries and current task health. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The S1 root can plan its work and delegate; Goal mode may automatically start the next turn, while fixed budget and session policy enforce caps; neither supplies a distinct whole-current S3 owner. - Decision owner: Current coding agent for task choices and Go runtime/operator for task caps, no independent supervisory S3 decision right. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: goalRun budget/verification, per-session dispatch mutex, cost guards, orchestration tool, per-subagent S2 semaphore, advisor diagnostic. - Closure path: Current goal turns and local enforcement update the same task stream; no distinct whole-current negotiated resource commitment or cross-unit managerial intervention is returned. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Goal continuation and current task planning are within S1; provider concurrency protection is S2, while independent turn review is S3, not by aggregation S3. - Evidence: internal/agent/goal.go, internal/agent/coordinator.go, internal/agent/agent.go, internal/agent/orchestratetool.go - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Model-owned separate read-only second opinion on a completed producing agent turn with concrete code inspection and corrective advice to next turn. - Claim being audited: Producer&#39;s final coding response satisfies user request and any source-code outcome it asserts is substantiated. - Ordinary reporting path: Main coding agent&#39;s user-facing final answer and its ordinary tool results; this path may be mistaken or overly confident. - Complementary access path: Optional configured advisor role builds a separate model invocation with only glob, grep, ls, view read-only tools; it can inspect actual working tree instead of relying solely on the producer summary. - Independence boundary: Advisor is a distinct model-driven checking pass, and optional escalate role can independently inspect code for flagged concerns. It shares the same first-party runtime and configured provider ecosystem, so not an externally or organizationally independent reviewer. - Who acts on findings: Advisor injects NIT/CONCERN/BLOCKER note through advisorNotes into the next main prompt; concern/blocker can also notify the user. The next producing Agent decides corrective action, not a hard mandatory merge gate. - Disturbance / variety regulated: False completion claims and unintended code changes missed by the production turn. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Second model chooses whether, which files, and how to inspect; judges severity and writes concrete issue for the next coding turn. - Decision owner: First-party model-driven Advisor, so A where advisor is enabled and role resolved; escalation may use another model without changing main owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Role-specific read-only tool list, result truncation, cadence EveryNTurns, timeout, severity parser, session note queue and optional user notification. - Closure path: Completed turn → advisor raw-source inspection/judgment → stored concern → injectAdvisorNote into next root turn → subsequent corrective work or user-visible warning. The pass is advisory/fail-open, so no forced correction guarantee. - Boundary reachability: coordinator.buildAgent constructs advisor only in ordinary non-subagent session when options.advisor.enabled and a model role are configured. sessionAgent.Run launches it asynchronously after turn; next Run injects note. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The audit judgment and decision to invoke raw code tools depend on the advisor model, unlike a static test or heuristic. The runtime only schedules and transports its finding. - Evidence: internal/agent/coordinator.go, internal/agent/coordinator.go, internal/agent/advisor.go, internal/agent/agent.go, internal/agent/agent.go - Basis: explicit / structural - Confidence: high for reachable conditional path; medium for actual audit efficacy - Caveats: Optional mode may be disabled by default, timed-out, skipped by cadence, or conclude NONE. Advisor report is not mandatory correction enforcement, and the goal judge&#39;s transcript-only confirmation is not treated as independent raw-source auditing.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No bounded source proof of a future-external environment sensing, strategic option selection and accepted change to organization-wide future capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Provider failures, historical prompts, memory and current goal continuation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Goal mode pursues a current user objective; model fallback and memory change execution continuity, not a prospective autonomous future operating policy. - Decision owner: User/configuration and ordinary task agent for model choices, no first-party prospective adaptation body. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Memory/factstore, context summary, fallback models, goal progress, templates and docs for future features. - Closure path: Existing context and provider choices may be applied inside current tasks, but no externally sourced prospective capability option is adopted into governance/operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Persistence, task resume, failover and alternate models do not imply an S4 intelligence loop. - Evidence: internal/agent/goal.go, internal/agent/memorystore.go, internal/agent/modelfallback.go, internal/agent/coordinator.go - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No autonomous or first-party parent-governed ultimate-policy/identity issue-deliberation with legitimate authoritative return at this agent recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unsafe shell use, cost overrun, tool permissions and user preferences. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Developer/operator configures permissions/model roles; runtime gates apply them locally; user approves or rejects current actions. - Decision owner: External user and fixed local policy enforcement, not an S5 governance organ with issue and binding policy decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Permission approvals, budget/session caps, tool access profiles, prompt templates, CLI configuration. - Closure path: Individual tools are authorized or denied; there is no identity-level dispute → legitimate authority → binding revised policy return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Configured safe execution is not self-governing identity-policy choice. - Evidence: internal/agent/coordinator.go, internal/agent/agent.go, internal/config/config.go - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f37ef211c66e5b5748caf83479e9ffce387f19dc/assessments/atlas-agent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f37ef211c66e5b5748caf83479e9ffce387f19dc\">f37ef21 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-05T04:11:40+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/AMD-AGI/PrimusClaw\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PrimusClaw</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-05\">2026-10-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/AMD-AGI/PrimusClaw/tree/bbd925a5b12b4ffe425c5ff01f00e9bc93863dfc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision bbd925a5b12b4ffe425c5ff01f00e9bc93863dfc\">bbd925a &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute autonomous coding/session work through a model-driven tool feedback loop in a per-session workspace. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous coding goals, repository/filesystem state, command/tool outcomes, model responses, sandbox failures/recovery, context limits, connected MCP/A2A capabilities and user interruptions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next reasoning/tool/sub-agent action from current context and returned evidence, revise work after results, and decide when the requested operational outcome is complete. - Decision owner: the active model-backed Brain agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: API session/task dispatch, Brain agent loop, tool router, Hands MCP tools, sandbox/workspace lifecycle, checkpoints, run locks/leases, model routing and deterministic execution/safety gates. - Closure path: user/task context → Brain agent chooses action/tool/sub-agent → Hands/MCP/runtime returns evidence → evidence returns into the model conversation → subsequent action changes until completion, interruption or bounded failure. - Boundary reachability: the standard Claw deployment directly instantiates API→Brain→Hands execution; no adjacent development system or third-party coding-agent internals are required for this S1 loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model actor leaves dispatch, storage, locks and tools but removes the open-ended coding decisions that transform observations into subsequent actions. - Evidence: README; claw/packages/brain/src/agent/agent-loop.ts; claw/packages/brain/src/agent/sub-agent.ts; claw/packages/brain/src/tools/router.ts; claw/packages/hands/src/tools/. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external model inference and SaFE sandbox placement remain dependencies rather than owners of the coding decisions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no distinct first-party S2 relation was established that specifically attenuates an evidenced peer-S1 interference or oscillation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: shipped in-process sub-agents may run concurrently and share the parent&#39;s workspace, while DAG dependencies and queue ordering sequence work; neither path demonstrates peer mutual-adjustment or collision regulation at the assessed recursion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no S2-specific decision over a concrete inter-S1 conflict was found. - Decision owner: not established at S2 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: sub-agent tool whitelists/depth limits, shared Hands sandbox, DAG dependency state, queue ordering, task locks and admission ceilings. - Closure path: delegation and DAG scheduling alter execution order or capacity, but no shipped relation specifically detects/attenuates an inter-S1 disturbance and returns that coordination result into peer behavior. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: sub-agent delegation, shared state and scheduler sequencing are not S2 without the Profile&#39;s specific disturbance/attenuation witness. - Evidence: claw/packages/brain/src/agent/sub-agent.ts; claw/packages/brain/src/agent/agent-loop.ts; claw/packages/api/src/tasks/scheduler.ts; draft claw/docs/agent-team-design.md inspected but excluded. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the draft AgentTeam design describes future coordination surfaces but is not shipped evidence at this ref.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: regulate current fleet-wide execution commitments against shared run, sandbox and GPU-node capacity, dependency readiness and task priority. - Disturbance / variety regulated: several queued/running task or DAG commitments can simultaneously demand limited execution slots, sandboxes and GPU nodes; failed dependencies and dispatch stalls can also leave current work in inconsistent states. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide which currently ready/queued rows are admitted/reserved for execution now, which remain deferred, which dependent work is failed, and when DAG roots can settle from peer status. - Decision owner: constructor/runtime policy. The API scheduler deterministically executes configured priority, dependency and admission-ceiling rules; no autonomous agent owns the fleet-wide admission judgment in the standard path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: current usage snapshots, run-root accounting, hard/soft admission ceilings, owned admission lock, PostgreSQL row locks/CAS, queued→preparing reservation, dependency promotion/cascade, dispatch timeouts and periodic scheduler ticks. - Closure path: current occupying/executing usage + ready/queued rows → scheduler applies dependency/priority/capacity policy → accepted rows transition/reserve and dispatch, rejected rows stay queued/waiting → later task status and usage change → next scheduler tick re-evaluates the current portfolio. - Boundary reachability: claw/packages/api/src/tasks/scheduler.ts and its admission machinery are shipped API-runtime paths; deployments can configure the first-party fleet ceilings without writing a new scheduler. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the whole-current resource-control function is concrete and closed, but its decisive policy is deterministic/configured rather than autonomously chosen or revised by an agent; therefore the publication state is constructor C, not A. - Evidence: claw/packages/api/src/tasks/scheduler.ts; claw/packages/api/src/tasks/admission.ts; claw/packages/api/src/config.ts; task/DAG DB lifecycle. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: zero-valued ceilings disable some capacity dimensions; the positive constructor claim concerns the shipped configured-ceiling mode.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no distinct complementary audit path over current S1 operational claims was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repository CI/tests, runtime events, skill-evolution mutation verification and safety checks provide various forms of validation, but none forms a separate shipped S3 path auditing ordinary coding operations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no operational auditor with materially different access to S1 reality and a separate audit-to-current-control feedback path was found. - Decision owner: not established at S3 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: event logs/metrics, task settlement checks, evolution mutation verifier, repository tests and safety validators. - Closure path: runtime checks remain ordinary execution/control feedback; the evolution verifier governs S4 adaptation proposals rather than independently auditing an S1 completion claim. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an autonomous verifier exists inside the skill-evolution adaptation path, but function-first mapping places that judgment in S4 capability adaptation, not S3 operational audit. - Evidence: claw/packages/api/src/marketplace/skill-service.ts; claw/packages/api/src/events/consumer.ts; task settlement paths; repository tests inspected as adjacent. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external evaluators or future team reviewer roles could create S3, but they are not credited here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: learn from cross-session environmental/task outcomes and autonomously generate, verify and install reusable skill adaptations for future operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: an active skill may underperform on changing user tasks or environments, evidenced by failures, high turn counts, repeated errors and contrasting successful/problematic trajectories across recent sessions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: determine whether observed bad cases justify a capability change, choose concrete skill-description/content/sub-file or new-skill mutations, and accept/reject those mutations through autonomous semantic verification before publication. - Decision owner: a distributed autonomous LLM path: the evolution LLM proposes evidence-grounded adaptation mutations and a separate verifier LLM can veto unsafe/weak mutations; deterministic validation and DB transaction machinery constrain and apply the surviving decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: shouldEvolveSkill statistics gate, cross-session good/bad trajectory retrieval, durable evolution-job queue/retries, mutation shape/path/size/safety checks, verifier score threshold, transactional batch application, probation/feedback state and active-skill selection. - Closure path: completed task outcomes and trajectories accumulate → degraded active skill crosses evolution threshold → LLM analyzes good/bad evidence and proposes future-oriented mutations → verifier/safety gates accept the batch → active skill content/files/version are updated transactionally → later tasks select that active skill → Brain prompt injects the evolved skill → subsequent S1 behavior changes. - Boundary reachability: the complete path is shipped in packages/api and packages/brain and is enabled through the documented first-party deployment mode CLAWSKILLEVOLUTIONENABLED=true; no custom code or external adaptation service is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic thresholds and safety gates alone cannot invent the adaptation; removing the LLM adaptation/verification actors leaves evidence storage and enforcement but no semantic choice of capability mutation. - Evidence: claw/packages/api/src/events/consumer.ts; claw/packages/api/src/marketplace/evolve-worker.ts; claw/packages/api/src/marketplace/skill-service.ts; claw/packages/api/src/config.ts; claw/packages/brain/src/agent/prompt.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: skill evolution defaults OFF and must be deliberately enabled; A describes this documented supported first-party mode, not every deployment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity- or ultimate-policy-level closure was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: authentication, execution templates/config, feature flags, tool permissions, sandbox/security policy, admission ceilings and operator/admin controls constrain operation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party runtime path routes a genuine identity/ultimate-policy issue to a legitimate ultimate authority and returns that decision as governing system identity. - Decision owner: not established at S5 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: auth tokens/roles, environment configuration, feature flags, tool whitelists, sandbox policy, resource ceilings, admin routes and deployment controls. - Closure path: operators configure or gate concrete runtime capabilities/resources, but those decisions do not constitute an identity-policy issue/authority/return loop at the assessed recursion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: security/configuration and operator controls bound the harness but are not evidence of S5. - Evidence: README; claw/packages/api/src/config.ts; Brain sub-agent tool policies; deployment/auth documentation. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: project maintainers have repository governance authority, but development governance is adjacent to the deployed harness boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/00d4154c7f718914f7f1d816f6c8b167b6a64d0f/assessments/primusclaw.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 00d4154c7f718914f7f1d816f6c8b167b6a64d0f\">00d4154 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-22T02:46:05+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/glebis/pibot\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pibot</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-22\">2026-09-22</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/glebis/pibot/tree/350bf2c8263f31bcda33789c819329bb56f14c00\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 350bf2c8263f31bcda33789c819329bb56f14c00\">350bf2c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: proactively monitor personal-agent state and produce an owner-facing intervention, escalation or maintenance action without requiring a new user message. - Disturbance / variety regulated: due/near-due schedules, recent events, stale memory/persona/consolidation state, owner inactivity, unresolved activity worth surfacing, quiet-hour/snooze constraints and uncertainty about whether an interruption is worthwhile. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide on each eligible heartbeat whether to stay silent, speak to the owner, escalate into the full companion brain, record a maintenance/backlog action and/or change the next wakeup interval. - Decision owner: the ephemeral model actor running inside the pibot-defined heartbeat protocol. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: scheduler timer, shouldTick guards, bounded digest, heartbeatact schema, wakeup clamps, event log, unanswered-speak backoff, transport delivery and Pi&#39;s generic session execution substrate. - Closure path: pibot timer/guard → pibot digest → model heartbeatact decision → tickInner applies speak/escalate/maintenance/wakeup → owner or full companion receives the action and/or next scheduler cadence changes → later heartbeat observes updated events/state. - Boundary reachability: heartbeat is enabled by defaultManifest, PiBot.start() installs a heartbeat job for discovered agents, and src/index.ts wires HeartbeatEngine directly to pibot delivery/escalation/scheduler/event services. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the timer and clamps decide whether a tick may run, but once a tick runs the model has bounded discretion over whether and how to intervene. Pi executes the ephemeral model session but does not supply pibot&#39;s heartbeat purpose, digest, decision vocabulary or return path. - Evidence: src/core/heartbeat.ts, src/core/types.ts, src/core/bot.ts, src/index.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: ordinary reactive chat also uses Pi sessions, so it is not used by itself to import a generic Pi S1 claim into pibot. The positive mapping rests on the first-party heartbeat organization.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2-specific coordination path is established among pibot companion S1 units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the review looked for actual or structurally evidenced sibling-agent interference, contention or oscillation rather than treating messaging/handoff as coordination by name. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party path was established that detects a concrete inter-S1 disturbance, chooses an attenuating coordination response and feeds that result into later sibling behavior. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: agentmessage, agentask, handoff briefs, chat rebinding, scheduler ordering and per-agent runtime/session separation. - Closure path: sibling communication/delegation can alter task flow, but no disturbance-specific inter-S1 conflict → coordination judgment → changed subsequent S1 behavior loop was established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a companion can choose to message or hand off to another companion, but delegation/communication alone is not S2 and the source does not identify a shared collision being regulated by that path. - Evidence: src/plugins/agent-comms-plugin.ts, handoff/session routing in src/core/bot.ts, runtime separation in src/core/agent-manager.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: pibot clearly supports multiple durable agent units; the negative classification concerns the stricter S2 witness, not the absence of multi-agent interaction.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: regulate the current pibot installation on behalf of the whole by controlling active companion population/configuration, models/capabilities/rhythms and current scheduled commitments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: agents needing to be created/removed/reconfigured, model/capability/rhythm changes, excessive or unwanted proactive activity, schedule commitments requiring cancel/snooze/wake intervention and operational state visible through event/schedule surfaces. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide which companions exist, what runtime capabilities/models/rhythms constrain them and which current schedules remain active, paused/snoozed or cancelled. - Decision owner: the authenticated local owner/operator in the first-party dashboard/chat control mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dashboard forms, manifest persistence, AgentManager.discover, Scheduler state/timer wheel, PiBot startup/rearm logic, authentication/CSRF controls and event/schedule presentation. - Closure path: installation/schedule/agent state → owner inspects dashboard/control surface → owner edits manifest/rhythm/capability or acts on schedule/agent state → first-party files/scheduler state update → pibot discovery/runtime/scheduler applies changed constraints to subsequent operation. - Boundary reachability: the authenticated dashboard is started by default unless explicitly disabled, is wired to the live AgentManager, Scheduler, EventLog and EvolutionEngine, and README documents the same agent/schedule/runtime controls as supported user-facing operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: pibot&#39;s host enforces saved manifests and schedule state, but no autonomous supervisor chooses the installation-wide current-control decision. The legitimate owner does, so the only positive published mode is parent-governed. - Evidence: src/web.ts, src/core/bot.ts, src/core/agent-manager.ts, src/core/scheduler.ts, README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: operational model responses, handoffs and heartbeat choices remain local S1 decisions. They are not used as S3 evidence. - Whole-system current view: the first-party dashboard exposes the installation&#39;s agents and per-agent manifest/rhythm, schedules, event tail and evolution state, providing the operator a cross-installation route to current operational state. - Current-control decision scope: agent participation, model/capability/rhythm constraints, current schedule activation/cancellation/snooze/wake and related intervention over live installation commitments.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: provide a narrow first-party complementary check of a companion&#39;s claim that it actually updated durable memory/MEMORY.md during heartbeat maintenance. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a heartbeat can report a maintenance action such as &quot;updated/refreshed MEMORY.md&quot; even when no corresponding filesystem write occurred; relying only on the agent&#39;s maintenance note would preserve a false operational claim. - Decisive decision or feedback right: determine independently whether the claimed write is corroborated by raw filesystem modification time, record a verified/unverified audit result and expose that result to later heartbeat state. - Decision owner: the shipped audit primitive is deterministic (verifyMemoryClaim); no autonomous audit actor owns the verification judgment, so the function is published as C rather than A. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: maintenance journal, filesystem stat, claim-pattern matcher, event logging and maintenance-panel rendering. - Closure path: heartbeat maintenance claim → deterministic raw-filesystem verification → verification record appended to maintenance journal → later maintenance panel surfaces verified or UNVERIFIED → subsequent heartbeat model can see the discrepancy and change maintenance behavior. - Boundary reachability: recordMaintenanceNote invokes verifyMemoryClaim for qualifying heartbeat maintenance claims in the shipped heartbeat path, and buildMaintenancePanel reads the resulting verification record into subsequent standard heartbeat digests. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the independent evidence channel is real and closed, but the audit judgment itself is made by deterministic host code rather than an autonomous auditor. The first-party path is function-specific and usable by the subsequent agent, satisfying the constructor publication boundary. - Evidence: src/core/heartbeat.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: this is intentionally a narrow S3 path; it does not imply a general independent audit of all companion outputs or external actions. - Claim being audited: the heartbeat&#39;s durable maintenance assertion that MEMORY.md was just seeded/refreshed/updated/written. - Ordinary reporting path: the model-provided maintain text recorded in the maintenance journal/event log. - Complementary access path: direct filesystem modification-time inspection of memory/MEMORY.md, independent of the model&#39;s textual claim. - Independence boundary: the verifier reads host filesystem metadata and does not accept the model&#39;s maintenance note as proof; missing/stale files generate an explicit unverified result. - Who acts on findings: the next heartbeat model receives the verification status in its maintenance panel; pibot supplies this feedback path, while autonomous corrective judgment remains to the receiving companion.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: learn from recent operational/environmental history, identify a recurring capability gap, generate and test a reusable skill adaptation and return successful adaptations into future companion capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: recurring user/task irritations, unresolved improvement-backlog items, recent events and consolidated lessons showing that the current skill repertoire is inadequate or inefficient for future similar work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose what skill to create/patch, propose the adaptation and probes, judge probe outcomes, and in autonomous mode accept a safe sufficiently successful candidate for promotion into live future capability; in parent mode the owner decides staged-candidate promotion/rejection. - Decision owner: autonomous mode is a distributed model arrangement — proposal and probe/judge model actors supply the adaptation and evaluation judgments, while deterministic gates/thresholds enforce safety and promotion policy. Parent mode is the authenticated owner reviewing staged candidates. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: event/consolidation input, improvement backlog, deterministic content/risky-pattern/stagnation guards, staging directory, probe runner, average-score threshold, live skill directory, git checkpoint, event log and announcement. - Closure path: user/environmental operating history → consolidated/backlog distinctions → model proposes future skill change → probes execute with candidate loaded → model judge scores → safe score ≥4 auto-promotes to live skills/ → AgentManager includes live skills in subsequent sessions → future operation changes. Parent mode replaces the automatic acceptance decision with owner promote/reject over a staged candidate. - Boundary reachability: EvolutionEngine and its LLM-backed IO are constructed in src/index.ts; PiBot.start() schedules evolution for manifests with evolution.enabled; the engine writes promoted skills into the same per-agent live skill tree that AgentManager loads into standard sessions. Dashboard/chat review surfaces expose staged candidates to the owner. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic gates decide only whether a proposal satisfies fixed structural/risk/score constraints. The content of the adaptation and its probe-based model evaluation are agentic; when they pass, the first-party runtime closes activation without a human. Separately, staged review gives the legitimate owner a parent-governed acceptance mode. - Evidence: src/core/evolution.ts, src/core/agent-manager.ts, src/core/backlog.ts, src/core/consolidation.ts, src/core/bot.ts, src/index.ts, src/web.ts, README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: evolution is opt-in per agent rather than enabled in defaultManifest; it is nevertheless a complete first-party supported operating mode. Generic memory consolidation alone is not credited as S4. - External distinction: repeated owner/task/environment outcomes recorded as events, maintenance backlog items and consolidated operational lessons reveal capability gaps. - Future / prospective distinction: whether a new or patched reusable skill would improve later tasks beyond the current run. - Adaptation option generated: a concrete staged SKILL.md create/patch proposal with rationale and task-specific eval probes. - Path back into current capability / S3: successful promotion writes the candidate into the live per-agent skills/&lt;name&gt;/SKILL.md tree; subsequent standard sessions load those live skills through AgentManager.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: establish and revise a companion&#39;s durable identity/purpose at the parent recursion and return that decision into subsequent pibot operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: an under-specified or changing companion role, tone and proactive stance requiring legitimate owner choice about what the companion is for and how it should present/act as that identity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide the companion&#39;s job/purpose, vibe/persona and proactivity identity settings, including answering ambiguity-gate clarification questions when the draft identity is too vague. - Decision owner: the human owner/operator using the first-party creation wizard or authenticated persona editor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: scorePersonaAmbiguity, QuestionBus, buildPersona, buildManifest, createAgent, AGENTS.md/agent.json persistence and DefaultResourceLoader context loading for subsequent sessions. - Closure path: new/ambiguous identity specification → pibot asks the owner for job/vibe/proactivity and, when needed, clarifications → owner answers/edits → pibot writes durable AGENTS.md + manifest → later companion sessions load that persona/runtime identity → subsequent operation follows the returned identity decision. - Boundary reachability: both the chat /newagent wizard and authenticated dashboard are shipped operator surfaces; AgentManager creates/discovers those runtime directories and loads the persisted persona into standard agent sessions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: pibot can score identity ambiguity and build the resulting files, but the legitimate owner supplies the authoritative identity choices. No first-party autonomous actor is evidenced as holding ultimate authority to redefine the companion&#39;s purpose. - Evidence: identity wizard in src/core/bot.ts, src/core/agent-factory.ts, persona form/update in src/web.ts, session loading in src/core/agent-manager.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: ordinary capability permissions, schedule approvals and task questions are not used as S5 evidence. The mapping is specifically the durable companion identity path. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: what a companion is for, its enduring persona/tone and whether it should be proactively active or reactive-only. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: parent owner/operator only. - Return-to-operation path: owner identity choice → persisted AGENTS.md/manifest → AgentManager resource/session loading → later companion reasoning/heartbeat operates under that identity.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/de708b8c91a353fd3bb60aaa87ce3f709fbe6d19/assessments/pibot.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit de708b8c91a353fd3bb60aaa87ce3f709fbe6d19\">de708b8 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-30T16:25:42+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/liiiiiiiiil/coding-agent-from-scratch\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Agent From Scratch</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-30\">2026-10-30</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/liiiiiiiiil/coding-agent-from-scratch/tree/cd7ec0ee2031d1723e1d38593d43385778fd5d30\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision cd7ec0ee2031d1723e1d38593d43385778fd5d30\">cd7ec0e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform task-specific software engineering, including investigating code, editing project files, running tests/commands and iterating on observed failures. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown repository structure, incomplete user requirements, file/content changes, command/test failures, tool errors, recovery and context limitations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next code/tool/plan action and revise behavior after each observed tool result. - Decision owner: the model-backed parent Agent executing through AgentRuntime and ParentRuntimePolicy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: file/shell/search tools, tool registry/permission gate, plan state, execution generations, recovery/checkpoint machinery, context budget, traces and sessions. - Closure path: user task and workspace → model-selected tool/action → first-party execution against the codebase → result/failure and state returned to model context → next corrective/continuation action. - Boundary reachability: standard python -m miniagent task execution binds the parent runtime to enabled file/shell/plan tools; model inference is externally provided but the operative action/feedback loop is first-party. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model actor leaves a deterministic tool executor and protection machinery without an agent able to choose problem-specific code modifications or react semantically to tests. - Evidence: runtime.py; agent.py; tools/file.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: permission prompts can pause an unapproved side effect; a supported preauthorized configuration closes this path without repeated human decision. No live provider E2E was run for this review.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material inter-S1 interference attenuation loop established at the coding-task recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: parallel observations, child task overlap, or concurrent shell calls may require scheduling and isolation, but the packaged children are read-only investigators and not competing mutating S1 units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not evidenced for a plurality of mutating S1 units. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: child concurrency and aggregate budgets, fixed role/tool/scoping restrictions, ordered result delivery, task/process lifecycle controls and plan-step serialization. - Closure path: no evidence of independent mutating S1 cells whose operational conflicts are identified and attenuated through a feedback relation. Read-only isolation and ordered tool results protect execution but do not prove S2 at this recursion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: generic parallel calls, single-parent work, restricted child investigations and sequencing are not a separately regulated inter-S1 coordination function. - Evidence: tools/delegation.py; delegation.py; runtime.py. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: source implements deliberate safety isolation for read-only children; that is valuable but not the inter-operational attenuation required here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no whole-system discretionary current-commitment/resource control loop over a population of operational S1 cells. - Disturbance / variety regulated: plan drift, failed tools, active processes and unfinished background child work are monitored or gated, but within a single parent task rather than organization-wide current S1 priorities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no actor controls the current commitments of multiple semi-autonomous operational units based on a whole-system view. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ParentRuntimePolicy, plan revisions and reminders, task/state tracker, durable tool boundary, child cancellation/result claiming, process cleanup, recovery rules and budgets. - Closure path: the parent can change its own plan, cancel a read-only investigation or wait for a process; there is no live multi-S1 overview → discretionary resource/priority judgment → changed commitments across an operational population → feedback loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the parent is an autonomous coding actor (S1); its self-management and deterministic task guards should not be relabeled whole-system S3. - Evidence: agent.py; state.py; tools/delegation.py. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: planning, cancellation and recovery are substantial first-party controls; the missing element is the whole-system recursion/decision scope, not an absence of safeguards.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge a coding result with a separately instantiated read-only reviewer which re-reads source evidence and reports concrete defects/regression risks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unnoticed implementation defects, incorrect assumptions, missing boundary checks and regression risks in the parent agent&#39;s engineering decisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a model-backed child reviewer selects defects/claims and supporting observed evidence independently of the implementer&#39;s own self-report; parent retains corrective action rights. - Decision owner: the separately instantiated reviewer SubagentRunner, scoped by the built-in reviewer profile. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model-callable delegatetask / spawnsubagent, built-in frozen reviewer prompt and read-only allowlist, child AgentRuntime, independent state/context and model binding, evidence-backed SubagentResult, background status/result/follow-up tools. - Closure path: parent implements a change → model autonomously delegates source review to agentprofile=&quot;reviewer&quot; → child reads/searches source using its own model/tools → evidence-linked findings return to parent through a tool result → parent can repair/retest or order follow-up before final acceptance. - Boundary reachability: the parent registry exposes both delegation tools in the normal model-facing runtime; AgentProfileCatalog ships reviewer by default; SubagentRunner constructs a new context/tool executor without relying on an external review service. - Claim being audited: the correctness and regression safety of the current coding implementation or task result. - Ordinary reporting path: parent model&#39;s own file/command results, plan progression and terminal task response. - Complementary access path: a separate role-specific model loop performs direct scoped file reads/grep/listing and must link claims to actual observed tool output; it need not accept the parent&#39;s implementation narrative. - Independence boundary: independent child state, context, prompt, role, execution budget and read-only tool enforcement; model provider may be the same family, and the parent supplies goal/scope and selected facts, so this is organizational/evidence-path independence rather than provider diversity or a mandatory external assessor. - Who acts on findings: the parent Agent receives synchronous results immediately or explicitly claims a background result; it decides subsequent corrections or verification, and may request another reviewer pass. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: fixed schemas can reject unsupported evidence but cannot identify arbitrary defects or their significance without the child model&#39;s substantive review judgment. - Evidence: agentprofiles.py; tools/delegation.py; delegation.py; agent.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: review must be chosen by the parent, not run automatically for every change. Read-only reviewer findings are not automatically promoted to independent verification state; they are complementary audit feedback for possible subsequent S1 correction. The separate benchmark grader is not credited for this positive runtime path.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party external/prospective organizational adaptation loop closed. - Disturbance / variety regulated: historical tool results, retrieved memories, skill definitions, benchmark outcomes and the author&#39;s lesson roadmap are available as context or offline artifacts, but no standard agent-owned response to a future environmental change is enacted as durable organizational capability adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no separate prospective intelligence/adaptation actor with authority to select and implement persistent changes to the operating system. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: explicit workspace memory editing and retrieval, context compaction, skill discovery, model configuration, plan revision and separately launched evaluation/ benchmark tools. - Closure path: memory retrieval feeds ordinary task context; benchmark reports remain external evaluation outputs; neither establishes sensing an outside future → generating alternatives → approving/enacting a persistent capability change → operational feedback. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: remembering a prior observation and changing the current task plan are S1 context/actions, not the required future-facing organizational adaptation. - Evidence: memory.py; context.py; evaluation/runner.py; English README. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: benchmarks may later inform human developers; no experimental result or unmerged roadmap feature is attributed to the frozen runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime ultimate identity/policy adjudication and authoritative return to the operating organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user/project instructions, permission policy, plan approval and child role restrictions constrain work, but do not form autonomous highest-level purpose or identity governance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no actor may legitimately redefine the agent organization&#39;s final mission, identity, or policy for subsequent work. - Decision owner: not established; highest-level purpose and permissions remain human/configuration-authored. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: project AGENTS.md injection, system prompts, permission policy/gate, CLI user approvals, fixed child profile contracts, plan approval and task stop rules. - Closure path: authored instructions/policies → enforcement/approval/denial during task execution; no operational identity conflict → ultimate-authority decision → authoritative organization-wide policy update/return loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model tool selection operates inside externally defined rules and cannot claim authority to rewrite them merely through planning or skills. - Evidence: permission.py; instructions.py; agentprofiles.py; state.py. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: ordinary user authority is important to the system&#39;s safety, but external human approval is not by itself an internally owned S5 loop under this assessment boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/d8bf4e22916e4d514bf2a762a5876dd8b5d0f04f/assessments/ag/agent-from-scratch.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit d8bf4e22916e4d514bf2a762a5876dd8b5d0f04f\">d8bf4e2 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-19T22:42:02+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Gen-Verse/Recuris\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Recuris</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-19\">2026-10-19</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Gen-Verse/Recuris/tree/7d3745ab787b1206ccd981cb1511100476097266\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7d3745ab787b1206ccd981cb1511100476097266\">7d3745a &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Select context-dependent task actions and responses using the configured underlying model, grounded task observations and currently installed skill memory. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Heterogeneous requests, uncertain model outputs, tool failures, state/intent ambiguity and incorrectly declared task completion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The model behind the supported Tau2Port.llmdraft chooses a tool call/answer based on delivered memory and actual turn context; checker bounces may cause a second model-generated answer, and grounded tool receipts feed later turns. - Decision owner: Configured downstream language model reached through RecurisAgent.generatenextmessage and TurnRuntime.runturn; the first-party memory manager/checkers are bounded scaffolding, not the owner of the model&#39;s next substantive task action. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Working-memory ledger, experiential memory card delivery, invocation rule ρ, receipt matching, checkers and sanitation, contextual draft and redraft, tau2 runtime adapter. - Closure path: New task/receipt → first-party grounding/working memory update → delivery/context → model draft including actual tool intention → checker/redraft/commit → environment produces new tool result → subsequent agent turn and answer. - Boundary reachability: The packaged RecurisAgent constructor loads skill memory, implements tau2 agent generatenextmessage, wires concrete Tau2Port model draft/commit methods to the exported first-party TurnRuntime; documented tau2/agent adapter runs this operating route. External tau2 provider is required for live execution, but the first-party integration is present. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Without the model, memory/rules/checkers can constrain and ground progress but cannot select contextual next actions and synthesize task responses from new observations. Removing the outer research evaluator does not eliminate this model-backed S1 operating path. - Evidence: src/recuris/runtime.py#L192, src/recuris/runtime.py#L227, src/recuris/adapters/tau2/agent.py#L81, src/recuris/adapters/tau2/agent.py#L189, src/recuris/adapters/tau2/agent.py#L247, src/recuris/adapters/tau2/agent.py#L284. - Basis: Source-integrated downstream model/tool result path, not empirical performance verification. - Confidence: High for model-facing wiring; untested provider/runtime availability. - Caveats: The underlying frozen model, simulator and original task-provider internals are external actors, not borrowed as additional Recuris metasystem functions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No distinct first-party interference regulator among independently viable S1 operating divisions with returned mutual adjustments is demonstrated at this memory-evolving single-agent recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Multiple hierarchical diagnosis workers can propose competing repairs; separate benchmark trials can disagree; ledger rules can select among them. These are experiment/proposal conflicts, not necessarily competing operational services. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Meta-agent diagnosis merges candidate memory patches within one future package, while deterministic gates admit changes based on tests; no distinct organization-level agent adjudicates concrete interference among two independently viable S1 service divisions and returns revised ongoing commitments. - Decision owner: Operator-selected meta-agent workflow and deterministic candidate gate; not a persistent S2 coordination mandate over an S1 federation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Hierarchical diagnosis workers/reducer, candidate plan schema, package lint, fingerprint, parallel task evaluation and memory-version serialization. - Closure path: Observed experiment failures → candidates consolidated → validation/gate → one revised memory package; no operating-S1 conflict detection → inter-divisional dampening decision → reciprocal S1 operational rights/behavior. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A model reconciling alternate experimental proposals contributes to S4 option generation; its presence does not make worker copies independently viable departments. - Evidence: src/recuris/metaagent/driver.py#L7412, src/recuris/metaagent/driver.py#L8268, src/recuris/metaagent/driver.py#L10310, src/recuris/metaagent/planschema.py#L1. - Basis: Function-first negative including hierarchical diagnosis and parallel validation paths. - Confidence: Medium-high at defined recursion. - Caveats: A downstream multi-agent deployment could add its own S2; it is not sourced here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No first-party agent executive owns discretionary present priorities, resource allocation and binding feedback across a portfolio of independently viable S1 divisions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Simulation budget, power limitations, stale candidate versions and task progress errors may threaten an evolution study; configured budgeting/round gates manage this run, not a current viable organization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Research operator chooses environment, trial counts, seed, splits, rounds, regression cap and gate policy; first-party Driver and budget accounting apply these settings, while the model selects task or proposed memory content under that configuration. - Decision owner: Operator/research protocol and deterministic run Driver; no independently mandated current organizational S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Generation profile/simulation budget, held-out power gate, run state and checkpoints, fail-closed plan acceptance, milestone/round/seed management and startup integrity checks. - Closure path: Model/task outcome → configured limit/gate/checkpoint controls → experiment retries, stops or continues; no whole-current managerial claim → discretionary change to ongoing independent S1 service commitments. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A ledger or manager-selected working-memory update cadence does not equate to managing separate live S1 organizations or their resource contract. - Evidence: src/recuris/metaagent/driver.py#L682, src/recuris/metaagent/driver.py#L4536, src/recuris/metaagent/driver.py#L10000, src/recuris/runtime.py#L192. - Basis: Source-bounded negative after checking both default runtime and research campaign. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: External research organization/benchmark owner may exercise genuine S3 but is beyond packaged runtime ownership.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independent complementary audit of the actual S1/S3 operational management picture with corrective findings returned to a separate current S3 authority is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: False agent completion, synthetic tool receipts, benchmark leakage, candidate causality errors and harmful patches are guarded; these are operational correctness and future memory-admission risks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Deterministic receipt grounding, checker bounces, paired held-out tests, fingerprint probes and leakage scans may veto current claims or future patches. No separate independent auditor challenges a sourced S3 management report and returns a correction to that manager. - Decision owner: First-party safety/checker/gate algorithms plus external benchmark scoring oracle; not an S3 mandate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Truth checkers, GroundingKernel, skill-carrier probes, paired bootstrap gate, no-synthetic-receipt safeguards, ledger and audit receipts. - Closure path: Current draft or candidate memory → local check/reject/redraft or statistical acceptance → current task continues or future memory replaced; absent reality inspection → S3 management picture correction → new S3 current control decisions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Strict scientific independent validation of a proposed S4 change is not by itself VSM S3. The latter must serve a separate current S3 executive with a complementary operating-reality challenge. - Evidence: src/recuris/runtime.py#L192, src/recuris/grounding.py#L1, src/recuris/metaagent/gates.py#L31, src/recuris/metaagent/reachability.py#L1. - Basis: Scoped negative despite substantial independent quality and safety assurance. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: These receipts could be inputs to externally designed S3 but do not establish first-party mandate/closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: Produce model-generated prospective changes to an agent&#39;s reusable skill-memory components from prior task failures and selectively adopt evidence-validated versions into subsequent downstream operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Systematic future task failure classes, memory carrier missing/incorrect invocation, weak procedural cards, task horizon effects and regressions across unseen held-out tasks. - External distinction: Driver.evaluate runs the currently installed memory over training tasks; failures, serialized/sanitized trajectories, mechanism fingerprints and differential evidence reveal operational inadequacies with respect to the environment. - Future / prospective distinction: A separately configured upstream model-powered coding meta-agent consumes evidence and formulates memory package changes E, W, invocation rule ρ or checkers C for later downstream runs, not merely a redraft of the same current task answer. - Adaptation option generated: A structured patch plan and concrete disposable candidate package (including memory cards, routing/checkers/working memory), tested for schema, source integrity, mechanism activation and leakage. This model&#39;s candidate is an option, not a self-authorized accepted policy. - Path back into current capability / S3: When deterministic paired held-out improvement/regression criteria pass, Driver.commit versions and adopts the candidate package; workingbasepackage and next-round downstream evaluation use the selected memory. Some rejected candidates may be only provisional working bases under explicit progressive rules, not falsely reported as canonically accepted. The return is to future S1 memory capability, not an independently established S3 executive. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model-backed meta-agent chooses future memory content; code-owned heldoutpairedgate, leakage/activation/repair rules and operator-chosen experiment constraints decide admission and subsequent test schedule. - Decision owner: Upstream configured model through shipped metaagent Driver/launcher and first-party candidate integration; acceptance is deterministic first-party code plus benchmark oracle. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Sanitized trajectories, diagnosis workers, candidate plan, lint/reachability check, gate with item bootstrap CI and regression cap, immutable checkpoints, committed memory manifests and future run loader. - Closure path: Downstream trial observation → model diagnosis/patch option → candidate sanity checks and paired held-out comparisons → admit (or reject/provisional) → selected version stored → next downstream run loads new memory → fresh task outcomes. - Boundary reachability: Documented recuris metaagent run starts the packaged Driver, phased/ phasep use configured upstream coding-agent launcher to create actual patches, gate checks comparable prior-vs-candidate evidence, commit updates the accepted memory base and subsequent evaluate loads it for downstream TurnRuntime. No new application glue is invented. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Without the upstream model, deterministic evaluations and gates could accept/refuse a provided patch but could not generate semantically grounded memory repair from new failure traces. Without the code gate and adoption, the model&#39;s suggestions would be non-operational advice; the positive is this source-connected bounded composition. - Evidence: README.md#L380, docs/architecture.md#L79, src/recuris/metaagent/driver.py#L5898, src/recuris/metaagent/driver.py#L7412, src/recuris/metaagent/driver.py#L8268, src/recuris/metaagent/driver.py#L9381, src/recuris/metaagent/driver.py#L9420, src/recuris/metaagent/driver.py#L9677, src/recuris/metaagent/driver.py#L10350, src/recuris/metaagent/gates.py#L31. - Basis: First-party integrated prospective model-option→verified selective adoption→future downstream operation path; empirical gain not independently reproduced. - Confidence: High for source-connected loop, moderate for measured effectiveness. - Caveats: Meta-agent loop is explicitly run/experiment-budget opt-in, may reject all candidates, and neither benchmark success nor improvement is guaranteed. Human selection of tasks and policy remains external, and S4 positivity does not confer S3/S5 rights.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No internally legitimate, first-party deciding authority resolves ultimate organizational identity/purpose conflicts and returns durable binding policy to the assessed downstream agent organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Data leakage, fake evidence, benchmark invalidity and unsafe memory mutation create integrity concerns; these are guarded by specified constraints rather than autonomous policy-identity judgments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human experimenter sets allowed tools, gate thresholds and frozen oracle/benchmark assumptions; loader and deterministic code refuse invalid changes. Neither current model nor meta-agent is allowed to rewrite the constitution of its own acceptance gate. - Decision owner: External protocol/user and first-party deterministic enforcement, not a first-party legitimate S5 agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Immutable package identity and integrity digests, plan schema, leakage check, gate policy and tool/write restrictions. - Closure path: Invalid candidate/action → configured check stops/rejects → continue under original protocol; no ultimate purpose dispute → S5 authority → returned enduring policy revision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Hard-coded gate restrictions, locked benchmarks or a human operator&#39;s outside authority do not constitute a model-formed legitimate governance mandate. - Evidence: src/recuris/metaagent/gates.py#L1, src/recuris/metaagent/integrity.py#L1, src/recuris/metaagent/planschema.py#L1, src/recuris/runtime.py#L192. - Basis: Bounded negative through normal/opt-in model evolution and safety modes. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Research team/other deployment organization may hold genuine ultimate policy powers externally; unassessed at this recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/db78ce82e20dfcd122182b5d1432c4ec3904ce00/assessments/re/recuris.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit db78ce82e20dfcd122182b5d1432c4ec3904ce00\">db78ce8 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-12T14:50:47+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Shelra/ShelraCode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ShelraCode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-12\">2026-10-12</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Shelra/ShelraCode/tree/45f87d1f7f71d5f6bba07405167893fa74d7c2a4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 45f87d1f7f71d5f6bba07405167893fa74d7c2a4\">45f87d1 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Autonomously carry out uncertain coding changes with actual tool, test and environment feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown code structure, failing tests/builds, incomplete requirements, changing local tool results, user edits and runtime failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Root model selects coding tool/repair actions and re-evaluates observed tool/criterion evidence; in autonomous objective mode a model proposes plans and repairs subject to real host acceptance gates. - Decision owner: First-party model-driven Agent and objective runtime; the fixed kernel and independent tools constrain completion but do not themselves decide the appropriate code change. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool registry, typed shell/file actions, host verification oracle, attempt cap, task acceptance criteria, source/memory context and session persistence. - Closure path: User objective → model selects read/edit/test operation → host actually executes → environment result/acceptance witness returns → model repairs or reports verified/blocked outcome. - Boundary reachability: Shipped interactive/headless command and --autonomous invoke installed Agent or AutonomyKernel with native tool execution and verification/repair controls. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Execution receipts cannot invent task-specific code decisions or repair strategies without the model; the model exercises the decisive operational discretion. - Evidence: Agent loop; runObjective; autonomous repair loop; first-party verification closure tests. - Basis: explicit and structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No shipped function-specific mechanism attenuating observed conflict or oscillation among distinct operating S1 workers and returning corrected behavior to both was found. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Simultaneous children could select overlapping files/tasks; concurrency and background scheduling create potential contention but not by themselves a coordinated feedback loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Root model chooses child scopes; tools restrict background explore to read-only and kernel sequences objective tasks. No native inter-S1 conflict witness/arbitration decision is evidenced in supported modes. - Decision owner: Root coding model for its own task; fixed sandbox and child nesting limits for execution safety. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Child role restrictions, detached explore process, optional skills and static/ordered planning, cancellation and job state. - Closure path: Child calls execute and return reports to parent; no concrete two-worker conflict detection → coordination intervention → changed constraints returned to the affected S1 peers. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Read-only delegates, a queue of sequential tasks, execution restriction or a scheduler does not alone regulate actual S1-to-S1 interference. - Evidence: read-only background child manager; child mode selection; sequential plan; headless task scheduling. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No distinct whole-current managerial authority over a sustained plurality of operating S1s was demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Multiple project tasks, process/agent execution budgets and scheduled runs could require current organizational resource/priority arbitration; shipped code mainly organizes a single coder&#39;s objective or individual prompts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The coding model selects current steps, retries and delegates one bounded task; deterministic kernel controls time/budget and completion. There is no attributed manager deciding collective commitments across a continuously operating S1 portfolio. - Decision owner: Single current-task root Agent, human operator for CLI/schedule/approvals, deterministic objective kernel for step gating; none owns a proved full-team current control loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AutonomyKernel task plan, project decision checks, daemon schedules, task/delegate tool, budget counters and persistent objectives index. - Closure path: Task status/verification → coding agent changes next step. Missing whole-system worker/commitment observation → managerial allocation/intervention → feedback into multiple operating units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A long plan, a task queue, stop/retry logic and a generic orchestrator named kernel still operate the single user&#39;s coding objective. - Evidence: objective kernel; task phases; recurring prompt scheduler; independent child job. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: In the explicit built-in verify and UI-verifier modes, independently inspect primary project/running-application artifacts and return a challenging evidentiary judgment to the coding controller. - Disturbance / variety regulated: The implementing coder may mistakenly claim builds, tests or UI interactions worked without actually executing them, or may miss errors in rendered runtime behavior. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A separate child model selects repository/build/test/boot/browser and UI observation actions, observes their actual outputs, then reports failures/evidence. The parent can prevent acceptance or revise work based on this returned verdict. - Decision owner: Distinct provider-backed verify or ui-verify Agent in supported opt-in verification mode; deterministic host can apply sandbox/recipe and reject unsupported executions but does not choose the observed verdict. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runVerifyOrchestration, verify-detect recipe, Shuru sandbox, built-in screenshot/browser/command tools, child-context isolation, returned ToolResult and kernel verification/failed status. - Closure path: Root code/reported success → dedicated child independently reads/executes a real project build/test/boot/browser or examines rendered UI → evidence-bearing pass/fail/artifact report → parent Agent/kernel records verification and can block completion or choose repairs. - Boundary reachability: Shipped /verify/--verify path creates real separate verify child through runVerifyOrchestration and runTaskRequest; ui-verify has its own built-in model prompt and inspection tools. Both are supported distribution modes, gated by required Shuru/browser platform where applicable. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The independently executing reviewer model collects and interprets real source/runtime observations; passive test status or producer self-report alone is not the positive evidence. - Claim being audited: The implementing coding agent&#39;s claim that the requested change works and, for UI verification, that the rendered workspace meets the visual expectation. - Ordinary reporting path: Main Agent&#39;s own tool transcript, completion statements, acceptance criteria and host verification recognizer. - Complementary access path: Separate verify Agent directly executes installation/build/tests/boot and browser smoke interactions in sandbox; UI-verifier examines rendered state and screenshot/source evidence instead of only summary text. - Independence boundary: Fresh child messages/context and sandboxed/isolated tool execution with its own reviewer brief; this is a first-party operationally separate evidence path, not a cryptographically blinded review. - Who acts on findings: Parent coding agent/kernel receives the ToolResult, records failure/verification status and may issue further code repair, or reports that verification did not succeed. - Evidence: prepare and run independent verify; verified source/build/boot/browser requirements; child creation and root verify feedback; separate built-in reviewer prompts; /verify and --verify mode. - Basis: explicit and structural - Confidence: medium-high for optional reality-sampling mode; source-level reachability is clear, but execution cannot be inferred when Shuru is unsupported - Caveats: Verify is opt-in and platform-gated; ordinary same-model requirement audit within Agent completion gate is not credited as independent S3. No claim of a guaranteed correct or producer-blind reviewer verdict.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No first-party continuing outside-and-future intelligence process choosing organizational adaptation options with returned change in whole-current capability is evidenced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: External packages/APIs and future project requirements can evolve, but normal web research is recruited to the present coding task, while memory reflection derives from past local execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Agent chooses current-task research and writes changes; memory reflection model may propose local facts/skills, with deterministic promotion/human involvement. No distinct future-environment option selector owns a strategic adoption decision. - Decision owner: Active coding S1 for present work; human for manual skill promotion/config; no S4 organizational controller. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Web search/open, project episodic memory, gated reflection, skills compiler and local decision ledger. - Closure path: Current request/previous code outcomes → present project research or local reusable note → future prompt/tool context. Missing prospectively sensed external change, alternative capability appraisal and explicit S4→S3 adoption feedback. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Learning from local historical tool outcomes and maintaining skills improve S1 and do not, by themselves, model a changing outside-and-then environment. - Evidence: experience reflection; memory consolidation; research for current task; web evidence. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No ultimate identity-purpose policy arbiter balancing whole-current and future-facing organizational demands at the declared harness recursion is demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Human-approved project ADRs, safety approval and secrets restrictions constrain implementation, but do not necessarily govern the autonomous coding organization&#39;s constitutional identity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human approves/rejects local architectural decisions and controls permissions; running agent checks globs/commands to prevent incompatible project edits. This is protected work policy, not shown as a legitimate S5 organization-level purpose arbitration. - Decision owner: External human project owner for decisions; deterministic ledger/check tools enforce them in future coding tasks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Persistent decisions ledger, approve/supersede lifecycle, glob-scoped checks, user credential policies and command sandbox. - Closure path: Operator-approved project technical decision → ledger check guards the next code edit/acceptance; absent distinct organizational S3/S4 policy conflict and supreme identity arbiter with binding return into whole-system policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Human approval makes a binding project design constraint but not automatically VSM S5 constitutional control. No running Agent owns ultimate purpose. - Evidence: project decision persistence; decision checking; operator approve/supersede path; decision gate. - Basis: explicit and structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/eb479db705c155436a24fd5751a56074a43bd01a/assessments/shelracode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit eb479db705c155436a24fd5751a56074a43bd01a\">eb479db &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-22T22:29:51+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/CosmonautJones/mission-control\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Mission Control</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-22\">2026-09-22</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/CosmonautJones/mission-control/tree/9337744f7ec940234ecdc2936fa426d70598d26d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 9337744f7ec940234ecdc2936fa426d70598d26d\">9337744 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform bounded coding or other project work against an admitted Fleet/workflow objective through externally reasoning coding-agent sessions launched and governed by Mission Control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repository state, implementation uncertainty, tool/environment feedback, mission-specific requirements, failures and reviewer feedback encountered while completing a child objective. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the substantive task reasoning path and actions within the supplied goal/prompt, worktree and harness-policy envelope. - Decision owner: the autonomous external Claude/model worker launched by the first-party Fleet/workflow path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runClaudeCancellable, per-child worktrees/branches, persisted run state, cwd admission, timeouts, caps, budgets, harness hooks, approval escalation, quarantine prompt stance and cancellation. - Closure path: operator/controller admits a goal and child specification → Mission Control validates and spawns an external worker with --worktree → the model chooses task actions and receives tool/repository feedback → the worker produces a result/diff or terminal failure/escalation → Mission Control records the outcome for verification/synthesis/control. - Boundary reachability: Fleet and runnable Workflows are documented standard cockpit surfaces; fleet-runner.js directly invokes the external Claude CLI for each admitted child rather than relying on repository-development tooling. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the first-party runtime owns admission, lifecycle and constraints, but substantive task-local reasoning is performed by the external model actor and is reachable through the normal shipped Fleet/workflow path. - Evidence: README.md; apps/cockpit/server/fleet/fleet-runner.js. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: merely reading pre-existing ~/.claude sessions does not create S1 ownership for Mission Control. The positive finding is grounded in first-party Fleet/workflow launch paths that actually reach the external operational actor.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no installation-level S2 mutual-adjustment function is established from the reviewed standard distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple Fleet workers can execute concurrently and potentially target related repository work, but the inspected paths do not establish a concrete interaction-generated oscillation/interference among sibling S1 units together with a first-party feedback relation whose purpose is to attenuate it. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established at the installation recursion. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: separate worktrees/branches, concurrency ceilings, dollar/token-oriented budget structure, run-state persistence, child settlement derivation and post-settle synthesis constrain structure and resource use but do not by themselves satisfy the required S2 witness. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no installation-level S2 function was established, so there is no S2 decision right to classify. - Evidence: README.md; apps/cockpit/server/fleet/fleet-runner.js. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: worktree isolation is valuable preventive structure, but the reviewed path does not show disturbance feedback returning into subsequent sibling behavior. Adversarial worker-review feedback maps to S3, not sibling mutual adjustment, and synthesis occurs only after children settle.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C(P) - Function: regulate current installation-wide agent/Fleet activity, risk approvals and resource commitments using live cockpit/run state and intervention controls. - Disturbance / variety regulated: active workers can request risky tools, exceed desired cost, stall, loop, move in a wrong direction, need cancellation, or require an immediate stop across Fleet spawning. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose a current intervention — approve/deny an escalated action, steer a session, cancel work, engage/disengage the Fleet kill-switch, or alter the current run/policy configuration before launch. - Decision owner: base constructor mode — no resident autonomous installation supervisor is supplied; a downstream controller can be composed over first-party machine-readable state and intervention surfaces. Parent mode — the human operator/approver using the cockpit. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: live session/Fleet summaries, triage and risk classification, SSE/API state, approval write paths, append-only decision audit, session steering, cancellation, spawn ceilings, budget projection/latches, global kill-switch and boot reconciliation. - Closure path: current session/Fleet/risk/cost state is observed → downstream controller or human operator selects an intervention → first-party write/control path records and applies it → runtime/spawn/approval machinery admits, denies, steers, stops or prevents subsequent work → updated current state returns to the cockpit/API. - Boundary reachability: the standard cockpit exposes the Agents/Fleet/History surfaces and documented inline approve/steer operations; Fleet&#39;s runtime state, kill-switch and policy enforcement are first-party installed paths rather than maintainer-only controls. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic caps, budgets, anomaly flags and kill-switch enforcement execute a policy but do not decide when the installation should change course. The product exposes an S3-specific observation/intervention path without assigning installation-wide current discretion to a resident autonomous manager. - Evidence: README.md; apps/cockpit/server/fleet/fleet-runner.js. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Fleet&#39;s automatic settle/synthesis transitions, hard caps and anomaly detection are mechanisms within current regulation, not independent evidence of autonomous S3 decision ownership. - Whole-system current view: the cockpit presents live multi-project agent state, attention-ranked approval needs, Fleet run/child status, current cost/budget state and history/audit evidence at the installation boundary. - Current-control decision scope: risk approval/denial, live steering, run cancellation, emergency Fleet spawn stop/reset, and pre-run cap/budget/verification policy selection.\">C(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge a worker&#39;s completion claim against its branch diff and Fleet goal and return findings into bounded corrective work before final acceptance. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a worker may exit successfully while its produced change is incorrect, incomplete, regressive, destructive or out of scope relative to the admitted goal. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently issue approve or reject with reasons/rubric evidence; rejection can trigger a bounded worker redispatch, while sufficient approvals allow final worker success. - Decision owner: the fresh autonomous verifier model session, distinct from the worker that authored the candidate change. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: separate verifier child lifecycle, read-only quarantine stance, authorship-blind adversarial prompt, worker-branch diff access, structured verdict parsing, fail-closed malformed/error handling, verdict history, minApprovals, bounded maxRounds, budget gating, prior-rejection reason injection and deterministic bad-diff backstop. - Closure path: worker reports successful completion → Mission Control holds the worker in verifying → a new verifier session independently reads the worker branch/diff against the goal → verifier returns approve/reject → Mission Control records the verdict → reject reasons are injected into a subsequent worker prompt and the worker is redispatched while bounds permit, or the worker becomes terminal rejected; enough approvals close success → run state proceeds toward synthesis. - Boundary reachability: adversarial verification is a first-party Fleet policy (policy.verify) documented in the shipped cockpit and implemented directly in fleet-runner.js; it is not repository-development review for Mission Control itself. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the semantic completion judgment belongs to a separately spawned autonomous reviewer actor. Deterministic parsing/bad-pattern checks and lifecycle policy constrain how its verdict is used but do not replace the reviewer judgment. - Evidence: README.md; apps/cockpit/server/fleet/fleet-runner.js; apps/cockpit/server/fleet/verifier.js. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the LLM verifier is not treated as a security boundary; verifier.js supplies a deterministic backstop for a narrow forbidden diff pattern. That limitation does not remove the distinct complementary-review function or its corrective-return path. - Claim being audited: the worker&#39;s implicit claim that a successfully completed child result/diff satisfies the Fleet goal and is correct, complete, non-regressive and in scope. - Ordinary reporting path: the worker session exits and returns its own result/output to Fleet, which would otherwise settle the child as succeeded. - Complementary access path: a separately spawned verifier receives the goal and the worker branch, independently inspects git diff/output and returns a structured verdict. - Independence boundary: verifier is a fresh child/session, is placed in a read-only stance, and its prompt is explicitly blind to who authored the work and instructs it not to assume correctness. - Who acts on findings: first-party Fleet verdict routing records the result and either closes success, launches additional independent review, or injects reject reasons into the next worker round and redispatches the worker within configured bounds.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no installation-level S4 path is established from the reviewed standard distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the repository contains session intelligence, pattern/graph derivation, anomaly detection, friction logging and an assisted self-improvement policy, but the inspected paths do not establish the required external-and-future sensing loop that generates an adaptation option and returns it into installation capability/current control. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established at the installation recursion. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Pattern Intelligence, derived knowledge graph, model-assisted per-session analysis/recommendations, friction log, improvement backlog, retrospective cadence and learning-policy.yml proposal/apply permissions. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: historical/session-derived analysis and internal friction-driven improvement can support learning, but the reviewed standard path does not show a qualifying S4 organizational function to classify. - Evidence: README.md; packages/harness/AGENTS.md; packages/harness/.harness/learning-policy.yml; apps/cockpit/server/intelligence/analyzer.js. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a downstream organization could use Mission Control&#39;s history/intelligence as input to S4, but that would require evidence of the missing sensing/adaptation closure at that downstream boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no installation-level S5 identity or ultimate-policy closure is established from the reviewed standard distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Mission Control has explicit risk policies, approval requirements, mission readiness, caps, kill-switch controls and protected harness-policy changes, but these govern operational safety/current authority rather than deciding what the organization ultimately is or resolving competing metasystem demands at the identity level. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for an identity/ultimate-policy issue. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: risk-typed human approvals, append-only audit, danger-zone rules, human-approval-policy.yml, protected learning-policy categories, mission readiness and emergency stop/reset controls. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a human is deliberately the approver for consequential operations and protected policy changes, but generic human approval is not automatically S5; no reviewed path establishes identity-level ultimate authority and its return to operation. - Evidence: README.md; packages/harness/.harness/human-approval-policy.yml; packages/harness/.harness/learning-policy.yml; apps/cockpit/server/fleet/fleet-runner.js. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the result does not claim Mission Control lacks policy. It distinguishes operational/risk policy and parent approval from the narrower S5 function defined by the Profile.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c994fd0a7f9cbc5955777fb9cdb8f2d7f6cb90d0/assessments/mission-control.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c994fd0a7f9cbc5955777fb9cdb8f2d7f6cb90d0\">c994fd0 &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
